WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.840
introduce our first speaker, Gang Wang,

00:04.840 --> 00:07.520
which will be sharing about the directed evolution

00:07.520 --> 00:12.200
in non-human primates, which identifies the AAV capsits

00:12.200 --> 00:15.680
with the novel motive enabling efficient muscle

00:15.680 --> 00:19.680
targeting and reduced liver tropism.

00:19.680 --> 00:20.680
Sorry about that.

00:21.880 --> 00:25.080
Just before Gang starts,

00:25.080 --> 00:28.160
I just a reminder that no flash photography

00:28.160 --> 00:30.840
is allowed.

00:30.840 --> 00:35.840
And if you haven't connected to the west in this wall room,

00:37.280 --> 00:40.560
the password is ASGCT 2026.

00:40.560 --> 00:43.560
And without any further ado, welcome, Gang.

00:43.560 --> 00:44.400
Thank you.

00:44.400 --> 00:46.440
Thank you for the introduction.

00:46.440 --> 00:48.440
Good morning, everyone.

00:48.440 --> 00:51.640
I'm Gang Wang from Beyond Genes Therapeutics.

00:51.640 --> 00:54.440
It's my honor to be here today.

00:54.440 --> 00:59.440
I will introduce our capsaid engineering platform

00:59.480 --> 01:04.240
and we identified muscle targeting motive

01:04.240 --> 01:07.920
by this platform.

01:07.920 --> 01:12.920
And this motive can be effectively tuned

01:14.040 --> 01:16.480
for optimal liberty targeting.

01:20.880 --> 01:23.920
All these findings come from

01:23.920 --> 01:26.480
our platform, Dynamo-Lib.

01:26.480 --> 01:30.560
We have a capsaid libraries.

01:30.560 --> 01:34.040
We have a calibrator,

01:34.040 --> 01:37.400
the calibrated barcoded libraries,

01:37.400 --> 01:40.600
and in vivo validation system

01:40.600 --> 01:45.600
combined with the capture, the signal recovery system.

01:46.360 --> 01:51.120
This pipeline allows us to find the clinical lead.

01:52.120 --> 01:56.640
Parasite is our capsaid library.

01:56.640 --> 02:00.320
We have two distinct kind of library.

02:00.320 --> 02:04.000
One is a random peptide insertion.

02:06.120 --> 02:09.760
We are eight of AV9 capsaid.

02:10.760 --> 02:15.760
Another one is a rationally designed peptide

02:15.880 --> 02:20.440
derived from a cell surface binding protein.

02:21.320 --> 02:25.280
And we use a dual DNA packaging system.

02:25.280 --> 02:30.280
This system yield a very high viral titer of library

02:31.600 --> 02:36.600
and this also achieves very good high-fusility packaging

02:38.480 --> 02:42.280
with very low backbone contamination

02:42.280 --> 02:47.280
and excellent consistency of phenotype and genotype.

02:48.280 --> 02:53.280
A calibrator is our calibrated barcoded library.

02:55.320 --> 03:00.320
We use AV9 as internal standards

03:01.440 --> 03:03.800
with different doses.

03:03.800 --> 03:08.800
And then we create central curve

03:09.680 --> 03:12.960
based on the dosage of AV9

03:12.960 --> 03:17.680
and the in vivo t-show expression.

03:17.680 --> 03:22.520
And then all variants are mapped to this curve

03:22.520 --> 03:27.040
to calculate normalized for the change against AV9.

03:29.240 --> 03:33.280
In chemo, we label the different variants

03:33.280 --> 03:37.760
with distinct epitope tags

03:37.760 --> 03:42.220
for multi-level capsaid benchmarking in a single animal.

03:43.520 --> 03:48.440
This is our signal recovery platform.

03:48.440 --> 03:53.440
We named the capture with magnetic bits

03:54.720 --> 03:59.720
contracted with a virus-specific probe

04:02.340 --> 04:06.360
to recover the viral MRI.

04:06.360 --> 04:11.360
And this method facilitated the RT preparation

04:13.640 --> 04:17.600
from a large amount of NHP tissues.

04:19.240 --> 04:22.560
This is our integrated dynamo-lib.

04:23.760 --> 04:28.560
We integrated multi-librates, multi-root,

04:28.560 --> 04:31.400
and multi-tissue screening,

04:31.400 --> 04:36.400
all in a single NHP model.

04:36.640 --> 04:41.640
The aim is to maximize the data output per NHP.

04:43.080 --> 04:46.440
This strategy is very productive.

04:46.440 --> 04:48.640
From intravenous delivery,

04:48.640 --> 04:53.640
we identified mild-cap series targeting to muscle

04:55.240 --> 05:00.240
and the lead candidate targeting to deep brain.

05:01.720 --> 05:04.120
From intracecho delivery,

05:04.120 --> 05:08.040
we identified syn-cap series

05:08.040 --> 05:12.240
which can transduce almost all NHP

05:12.240 --> 05:13.440
looking at cells.

05:14.720 --> 05:18.600
Now we move to our discovery

05:18.600 --> 05:23.600
of muscle targeting motif.

05:25.560 --> 05:29.240
And this is the first round in vivo screening.

05:29.240 --> 05:31.960
At this early stage,

05:31.960 --> 05:36.960
we didn't find a reliable linear motif,

05:37.000 --> 05:42.000
but we found a very strong argument

05:43.240 --> 05:48.240
about the arginine accumulation across all clusters.

05:49.560 --> 05:51.680
In the second round screening,

05:52.440 --> 05:57.120
we eyes the selection pressure increase,

05:58.360 --> 06:03.360
R6, the arginine at the sixth position accumulated dramatically.

06:04.360 --> 06:09.360
And we then analyzed the round of the R6 anchor

06:10.560 --> 06:14.560
revealed that dominant PRT motif

06:14.560 --> 06:18.680
across all the muscles and the heart.

06:18.680 --> 06:23.680
And then we recasturing fixed R energy

06:25.120 --> 06:30.120
and we confirmed that it's the first round

06:30.520 --> 06:34.720
R energy and we confirmed that it's the PRG motif.

06:35.960 --> 06:40.960
And then we verified this motif in different species

06:42.520 --> 06:47.520
and we select four PRG variants

06:49.000 --> 06:53.400
and package them individually,

06:53.400 --> 06:58.400
label them with different IPP attacks

06:59.400 --> 07:02.760
and mix them together and then

07:02.760 --> 07:07.760
admit to animal by intravenous injection.

07:08.640 --> 07:13.640
And we tested them in Rhesus macaque

07:15.680 --> 07:19.440
SDRS and two species of mice.

07:19.440 --> 07:24.440
And all the PRT variants show a higher muscle expression

07:25.440 --> 07:30.440
than AB9 and the best one is BY087.

07:36.320 --> 07:41.320
And then we further validated BY087 in Rhesus macaque

07:45.000 --> 07:50.000
and at the dose of five times the power of 12,

07:51.000 --> 07:56.000
VG protege, BY087 outperforms every now across

07:56.720 --> 08:00.920
DNA, MRI and protein levels in all tested muscles.

08:02.360 --> 08:07.360
And since we have already verified the PRG motif,

08:07.840 --> 08:12.840
then we did the optimization of this PRG motif

08:13.200 --> 08:17.560
and the structure modeling by alpha fold

08:17.560 --> 08:22.560
reveals that there is a distinct protrusion

08:23.560 --> 08:28.560
introduced by SALTPRG insertion of BY087.

08:31.880 --> 08:36.480
We hypothesized, optimized the

08:36.480 --> 08:41.480
and terminal of the PRG motif could be

08:42.360 --> 08:47.000
maximized the muscle potency of the PRG.

08:47.000 --> 08:52.000
So we established the next round of HP screening

08:53.400 --> 08:58.020
by NNK saturation library.

08:59.960 --> 09:04.960
And the correlation analysis of the NNK library screening

09:07.160 --> 09:12.160
revealed that there is a very strong positive correlation

09:12.840 --> 09:17.840
between muscle expression and the liver expression.

09:17.840 --> 09:22.840
First, we identified the consensus sequence

09:24.080 --> 09:29.080
for muscle tropism, it is SAXY PRG.

09:30.440 --> 09:35.440
And then we focus on the afterlife population

09:36.000 --> 09:41.000
as shown in the right color with high muscle expression

09:41.880 --> 09:46.880
and low liver expression compared to the background.

09:48.480 --> 09:53.120
We found a distinct Hesidian accumulation

09:53.120 --> 09:58.120
in the second position and the fourth position.

09:58.160 --> 10:02.440
We named it the H switch.

10:04.680 --> 10:08.280
And then we developed our next generation

10:08.280 --> 10:13.280
male cap from white lab direct evolution

10:15.360 --> 10:20.080
and the structure informed design using H switch.

10:20.080 --> 10:23.520
And also the AI guide discovery

10:23.520 --> 10:28.120
to expand our candidate.

10:28.120 --> 10:33.120
Finally, we got 32 unique next generation variants

10:33.200 --> 10:38.200
and we tested them using our calibrator platform.

10:43.320 --> 10:48.320
The result shows most of the next generation variants

10:49.400 --> 10:54.400
show a higher muscle expression than BY 087.

10:55.960 --> 11:00.960
And the H switch works in the same way

11:03.680 --> 11:06.560
very well in these variants.

11:08.040 --> 11:12.400
When the double H incorporation

11:14.600 --> 11:19.600
achieves very high muscle liver ratio

11:22.280 --> 11:27.040
around 32 times and 23 times,

11:28.040 --> 11:33.040
it's much higher than the BY 087.

11:34.520 --> 11:38.520
It is only 3.8 fold.

11:38.520 --> 11:43.520
And the single H incorporation is also very effective.

11:46.720 --> 11:51.720
It reached the ratio of 17 fold.

11:52.000 --> 11:57.000
And then we selected the two kind of variants.

12:00.320 --> 12:05.320
The blue one is the muscle potency

12:05.760 --> 12:09.880
and the right one is the liberally targeting group.

12:09.880 --> 12:14.080
And for the right group,

12:14.080 --> 12:18.000
it's much published mile away.

12:19.000 --> 12:24.000
And this is the BY 438 result in calibrator.

12:26.080 --> 12:31.080
And the linear, we can see the linear regression of AV9.

12:33.240 --> 12:38.240
And also we can see the muscle expression here

12:38.360 --> 12:43.360
for the BY 488, much higher than AV9.

12:44.360 --> 12:49.360
And with liver, less expressed in the liver.

12:51.160 --> 12:56.160
And then we make higher to higher comparison

12:56.400 --> 13:01.280
between BY 438 and AV9.

13:01.280 --> 13:06.280
And we can see the BY 483 expressed much higher MRI

13:06.280 --> 13:11.280
in muscles than liver.

13:15.800 --> 13:20.800
And only 40% MRI expression in liver compared to AV9.

13:23.400 --> 13:28.400
The protein, the IF 10 confirmed protein expression

13:30.640 --> 13:33.080
consistent with MRI expression.

13:33.080 --> 13:36.760
This is the last presentation slide.

13:36.760 --> 13:41.520
We did cross species validation in Santa Mogas

13:41.520 --> 13:43.800
for myocardium variants.

13:43.800 --> 13:48.800
And the H switch works very well in Santa Mogas.

13:51.560 --> 13:55.000
It's actually for the double H incorporation.

13:57.080 --> 14:00.040
As shown in red color,

14:00.040 --> 14:05.040
the auto-perform the published control myocardium and VG7.

14:08.760 --> 14:13.760
For conclusion, Danily is end-to-end pipeline

14:14.840 --> 14:17.120
for calibrator discovery.

14:17.120 --> 14:22.120
And the PRD motif, so as a strong muscle targeting motif

14:25.160 --> 14:27.320
while flanking H residue,

14:27.320 --> 14:32.320
acts as a precise switch to abolish the level of targeting.

14:32.560 --> 14:37.560
The myocype series, like BY 438,

14:38.160 --> 14:43.160
demonstrate a special muscle transduction

14:44.680 --> 14:48.440
and hepatic escape in other HP,

14:48.440 --> 14:53.440
serving as ideal vector for muscle-related gene therapy.

14:53.720 --> 14:54.560
Thank you.

14:55.560 --> 14:56.560
Thank you.

15:00.560 --> 15:01.840
Thank you, Gang.

15:01.840 --> 15:03.840
Do we have any questions for Gang?

15:05.600 --> 15:06.760
We have one minute.

15:08.360 --> 15:10.040
I'm sorry, I may have missed it.

15:10.040 --> 15:12.320
Did you mention the receptor that you believe

15:12.320 --> 15:13.320
this is binding to?

15:14.400 --> 15:18.240
No, we are trying to find the receptor.

15:18.240 --> 15:20.600
Unfortunately, until now, we didn't find it.

15:21.600 --> 15:25.520
Hi, I'm nice to talk.

15:25.520 --> 15:28.920
You mentioned that you use dose calibration curve

15:28.920 --> 15:30.400
in your libraries.

15:30.400 --> 15:32.600
I'm curious how you use that data on the other end

15:32.600 --> 15:35.120
and what you've learned about dose response

15:35.120 --> 15:37.320
of various AV variants.

15:38.680 --> 15:42.160
You mean the mechanism of our calibrator?

15:43.080 --> 15:45.600
Yeah, like how you use the calibrator data?

15:45.600 --> 15:49.920
Yeah, we have our algorithm, calibrated algorithm,

15:49.960 --> 15:54.960
and we use the change fold,

15:56.320 --> 16:01.320
change fold to represent the potency of each variant.

16:02.640 --> 16:07.640
We get the T2 expression data,

16:11.520 --> 16:15.800
put them, might put them into the standard curve

16:15.800 --> 16:20.400
and then we will calculate the fold change

16:20.400 --> 16:22.280
compared to the standard curve.

16:22.280 --> 16:27.280
That means the increase fold against the AV9.

16:28.120 --> 16:29.440
Okay, thank you.

16:29.440 --> 16:30.360
Thank you, Gang.

16:35.360 --> 16:36.440
Thank you, Gang.

16:36.440 --> 16:39.600
With that, I would like to invite our next speaker,

16:39.600 --> 16:43.760
Benjamin, and he is my joint collaboration

16:43.800 --> 16:46.120
of the Tevira Therapeutics

16:46.120 --> 16:49.720
and the Department of Cellular and Molecular Medicine

16:49.720 --> 16:53.320
in Belgium, so welcome, Benjamin.

16:53.320 --> 16:56.720
Thank you, it's nice being here.

16:56.720 --> 16:59.160
So today I wanted to tell you a little bit

16:59.160 --> 17:00.840
about what we have been doing with Tevira

17:00.840 --> 17:02.560
over the last year and we've been working

17:02.560 --> 17:04.600
on a novel capsa platform,

17:04.600 --> 17:09.080
which we call the DEV platform, some of my disclosures.

17:09.960 --> 17:12.880
So I think I'm preaching to the choir here

17:12.880 --> 17:16.800
when I say that AV development is kind of hindered

17:16.800 --> 17:20.440
by the specificity and the potency of AV vectors

17:20.440 --> 17:23.040
and also the manufacturing can be sometimes

17:23.040 --> 17:24.400
always quite complex.

17:24.400 --> 17:26.680
So at Tevira, we wondered, okay,

17:26.680 --> 17:30.920
can we make this more streamlined and more directed

17:30.920 --> 17:33.040
by zip coding AVs to send them directly

17:33.040 --> 17:36.160
to specific tissues in a rational way?

17:36.160 --> 17:37.800
And the way we did that is through

17:37.800 --> 17:38.960
what we call our DEV platform,

17:38.960 --> 17:40.840
stands for Decorated AVs.

17:40.840 --> 17:42.920
What we basically did is we started

17:42.920 --> 17:45.920
with a parental AV serotype and in our case,

17:45.920 --> 17:49.280
we checked the AV2 and AV9, but we went ahead with AV9

17:49.280 --> 17:51.800
and we did some minimal vector engineering.

17:51.800 --> 17:56.480
What we did was we added in the surfaces post loops,

17:56.480 --> 18:00.320
we added a recognition tag for an enzyme called sortase A.

18:00.320 --> 18:03.360
And what happens is this sortase can recognize this tag

18:03.360 --> 18:06.960
and then when it does so it can attach targeting groups

18:06.960 --> 18:08.840
to the capsits and these targeting groups

18:08.840 --> 18:13.840
then afford specificity and potency of the vector.

18:13.840 --> 18:18.480
So as you see here, this tag is in all the viral proteins

18:18.480 --> 18:19.320
of the capsits.

18:21.160 --> 18:22.680
So here a little animation, how it works.

18:22.680 --> 18:25.520
You see the tag is in the pink loops.

18:25.520 --> 18:28.000
The sortase recognizes it and a targeting group.

18:28.000 --> 18:30.760
In this case, I'm just showing a VHH domain

18:30.760 --> 18:33.520
but can be anything, comes in, recognizes it

18:33.520 --> 18:37.400
and is covalently attached to the capsits.

18:39.800 --> 18:41.560
So how do we make it?

18:41.560 --> 18:43.560
So basically just make the AV first

18:43.560 --> 18:46.240
and we go to our up and downstream manufacturing.

18:46.240 --> 18:49.440
Then we do the conjugation in what we sometimes refer

18:49.440 --> 18:50.920
to as a shake and bake reaction.

18:50.920 --> 18:52.800
You just incubate it for a given amount of time

18:52.800 --> 18:55.840
at room temperature after which you purify out

18:55.840 --> 18:58.440
the enzyme in the non-reactive targeting groups.

18:59.440 --> 19:02.320
And that affords a very strong platform modularity.

19:02.320 --> 19:05.520
As I said, we can do with AV2, with AV9,

19:05.520 --> 19:09.880
we can target several surface exposed loops.

19:09.880 --> 19:11.400
Some of them work better than the other

19:11.400 --> 19:14.440
and we can target anything from small molecules

19:14.440 --> 19:18.440
to VHH domains on those capsits.

19:18.440 --> 19:21.000
And that makes that we have a predictable

19:21.000 --> 19:22.600
and reproducible product.

19:22.600 --> 19:24.040
So we have confirmed the structure

19:24.040 --> 19:28.680
of these conjugated molecules using mass spec.

19:28.680 --> 19:32.640
We can quantify them using Westerblot and Eliza assays.

19:33.560 --> 19:35.000
And when we do transduction assays,

19:35.000 --> 19:38.320
we see in vitro that it is nice and reproducible.

19:39.520 --> 19:43.040
So the first thing we did for just for the heck of it,

19:43.040 --> 19:45.520
I guess, was conjugating biotin to it.

19:45.520 --> 19:48.280
And what you can see here on the left-hand side,

19:48.280 --> 19:50.360
that is a non-conjugated AV vector.

19:50.360 --> 19:52.880
You see the VP123 bands.

19:52.880 --> 19:55.600
And then when we do the conjugation with biotin,

19:55.600 --> 19:57.920
if we just stain for the viral protein,

19:57.920 --> 20:00.680
we have basically the C terminal fragment.

20:00.680 --> 20:05.680
So that is downstream of the recognition side

20:06.160 --> 20:08.600
that you see here at the bottom.

20:08.600 --> 20:11.240
And that's the sort of cleft fragment.

20:11.240 --> 20:12.880
So that confirms the conjugation.

20:12.880 --> 20:15.280
But then when we also look at the biotin staining,

20:15.280 --> 20:17.280
that is attached to the N terminal fragment

20:17.280 --> 20:18.520
of the viral protein,

20:18.520 --> 20:23.520
you see that we conjugate both VP1, VP2N, VP3 of the capsits.

20:25.480 --> 20:26.880
So that's all really good,

20:26.880 --> 20:28.760
but we don't care too much about biotin.

20:28.760 --> 20:30.240
So we want to do cell targeting.

20:30.240 --> 20:32.560
And again, in the early days of this platform,

20:32.560 --> 20:34.520
when we just wanted to validate this,

20:34.520 --> 20:36.840
we used the HER2 binding VHH.

20:36.840 --> 20:40.360
And what you can see is in HER2 negative cells,

20:40.360 --> 20:41.880
we barely have any transduction.

20:41.880 --> 20:44.400
That's because we do the specific engineering

20:44.400 --> 20:46.520
that makes the capsit, basically,

20:46.520 --> 20:50.160
not transduced so well in any cell type.

20:50.160 --> 20:52.920
But then when we conjugate the HER2 VHH,

20:52.920 --> 20:54.760
we have specific targeting.

20:54.760 --> 20:57.440
Again, HER2, not super interesting for us.

20:57.440 --> 21:01.360
What we wanted to do is we wanted to reach the CNS.

21:01.360 --> 21:05.440
And we did that with, obviously, the transfer receptor,

21:05.440 --> 21:08.080
what so many others are doing as well,

21:08.080 --> 21:09.600
trying to piggyback on that receptor,

21:09.600 --> 21:11.320
shuttling across the anothelial cells

21:11.320 --> 21:12.840
to get into the brain prankima.

21:14.720 --> 21:15.760
And that's what you can see here.

21:15.760 --> 21:17.880
We started off with a mouse transfer receptor

21:17.880 --> 21:20.960
in mouse transfer receptor overexpressing cells.

21:20.960 --> 21:24.480
But crucially, we also went into human transfer

21:24.480 --> 21:25.480
receptor positive cells.

21:25.480 --> 21:27.800
And you can see that compared to NAV9,

21:27.800 --> 21:30.000
our constructs have, at least in vitro,

21:30.000 --> 21:32.800
an 18-fold improved transduction efficiency.

21:32.800 --> 21:36.040
Of course, again, we don't want to transduce

21:36.040 --> 21:37.280
HDFR positive cells.

21:37.280 --> 21:39.440
We want to cross the BBB.

21:39.440 --> 21:42.280
And so we did an in vivo bi-distribution study.

21:42.280 --> 21:46.560
So what we did is just with a GFP reporter gene

21:46.560 --> 21:51.560
and human transfer receptor knock in mice

21:52.760 --> 21:54.920
at those of 5V, 11VG per mouse.

21:54.920 --> 21:58.480
We injected this IV and we looked at the bi-distribution.

21:58.480 --> 22:01.360
So on the left here, you see AV9.

22:01.360 --> 22:06.000
That's kind of typical of what we would see at this dose.

22:06.000 --> 22:08.720
But then when we took our lead CNS, Dave Kapsit,

22:08.720 --> 22:10.400
you see that we have nice and widespread

22:10.400 --> 22:14.600
brain distribution, including the deeper brain areas.

22:14.600 --> 22:18.000
And if we zoom in a little bit here in the top row,

22:18.000 --> 22:21.400
you can see AV9 with a new end counterstain for neurons.

22:21.400 --> 22:25.440
And in the bottom row, you can see our Dave Kapsit.

22:25.440 --> 22:29.440
And again, you see that almost all brain regions

22:29.440 --> 22:31.220
are efficiently transduced.

22:32.600 --> 22:35.800
Of course, we want to quantify this.

22:35.800 --> 22:37.920
So here on the left-hand side,

22:37.920 --> 22:39.600
you see the VG copies in the brain.

22:39.600 --> 22:41.480
And you see that we have two logs

22:41.480 --> 22:43.520
improvement in brain targeting.

22:43.520 --> 22:47.360
And then when we look at the neuronal transduction,

22:47.360 --> 22:50.000
you see that we have anywhere depending on the brain region

22:50.000 --> 22:53.600
between 20% and 80% of the neurons transduced,

22:53.600 --> 22:56.760
which I think is quite important

22:56.760 --> 23:00.080
and strongly improved relative to, for instance, AV9.

23:01.520 --> 23:03.400
Very importantly, the liver, of course,

23:03.400 --> 23:07.620
if we go systemic with many Kapsits out there,

23:07.620 --> 23:08.880
you see they end up in the liver

23:08.880 --> 23:11.280
associated with liver tox.

23:11.280 --> 23:14.160
Our Dave Kapsits are, again, two log

23:14.160 --> 23:18.600
de-targeted from the liver on the VG level.

23:18.600 --> 23:20.680
And that's something that is inherent to our platform.

23:20.680 --> 23:23.200
So we see the same effect if we don't conjugate.

23:23.200 --> 23:26.080
So this is really due to how we do the engineering

23:26.080 --> 23:27.160
in the Kapsits.

23:27.160 --> 23:31.080
And basically these 100-fold improvement in brain targeting

23:31.080 --> 23:33.000
and 100-fold reduction in liver targeting

23:33.000 --> 23:36.280
opens up that therapeutic window with four logs.

23:38.360 --> 23:40.840
So that's on CNS.

23:40.840 --> 23:42.280
But of course, this is platform technology.

23:42.280 --> 23:43.880
We don't only want to target CNS.

23:43.880 --> 23:46.280
We want to go more broadly as well.

23:46.280 --> 23:49.240
There's a large need for Kapsits that target kidney.

23:49.240 --> 23:52.760
There's a large number of genetic kidney diseases

23:52.760 --> 23:57.760
with devastating outcomes and very limited treatment options.

23:58.120 --> 24:02.640
So we said, okay, can we make vectors, AV vectors,

24:02.640 --> 24:07.640
that more strongly target cell types in the kidney?

24:07.720 --> 24:10.200
And we focused on a little bit of polycytes.

24:10.200 --> 24:12.240
I'm not showing you that work today,

24:12.240 --> 24:15.640
but also we focus on tubular epithelial cells

24:15.640 --> 24:18.640
with a vision of treating tuberopathies down the line.

24:20.120 --> 24:23.320
And what you can see here, this is just in vitro data.

24:23.320 --> 24:25.920
We're still working on the in vivo work,

24:25.920 --> 24:28.440
but you see that again here compared to an AV9

24:28.440 --> 24:31.600
in cell culture, we have that 20-fold improvement

24:31.600 --> 24:35.680
in cell targeting with our ligand tree here.

24:35.680 --> 24:37.960
So we're still improving on that.

24:37.960 --> 24:41.040
We think that we haven't reached the ceiling.

24:41.040 --> 24:43.920
We can do some engineering there on both the Kapsits

24:43.920 --> 24:47.200
and the ligand level to improve that.

24:47.200 --> 24:50.600
And at the same time, Tevira is planning to put this

24:50.600 --> 24:52.920
in an in vivo study.

24:55.560 --> 24:59.480
So to summarize, the Dave platform that we have developed

24:59.480 --> 25:02.800
gives a modular design for designing potents,

25:02.800 --> 25:05.720
but also very specific AV Kapsits.

25:05.720 --> 25:08.600
It's something, these post-manufactured conjugation

25:08.600 --> 25:13.040
technologies, and I think we will hear some more about it

25:13.080 --> 25:17.560
in this session, that has a strong potential

25:17.560 --> 25:20.640
to rationally engineer AV Kapsits

25:20.640 --> 25:23.080
also in a human targeted context.

25:24.000 --> 25:27.120
Our platform can do that by enzymatic conjugation

25:28.080 --> 25:29.880
with an inherent liberty targeting.

25:31.040 --> 25:33.600
We have strong proof of concept for CNS targeting

25:33.600 --> 25:37.160
and then in vitro proof of concept in kidney targeting,

25:37.160 --> 25:39.280
which we will further work on.

25:40.400 --> 25:42.280
Of course, this is work that we haven't done alone.

25:42.280 --> 25:44.800
We developed the Dave platform was initially developed

25:44.800 --> 25:47.480
at the University of Leuven,

25:47.480 --> 25:49.440
which we whom we have a collaboration.

25:50.680 --> 25:55.480
And with that, I would like to thank you for your attention

25:55.480 --> 25:57.320
and take any question you have.

25:57.320 --> 26:02.160
Thank you Benjamin.

26:03.160 --> 26:05.760
