50:36.560 --> 50:39.080
Here's my disclosure slide, and my title slide.

50:43.480 --> 50:46.200
About 50% of the human population

50:46.200 --> 50:48.880
is ineligible for a gene therapy trial.

50:49.960 --> 50:54.960
And that is largely because of neutralizing antibodies

50:54.960 --> 50:56.120
that humans have.

50:59.040 --> 51:02.840
Actually, can we switch to the non-presenter mode?

51:02.840 --> 51:05.320
Just the slides, full screen, please.

51:05.720 --> 51:06.560
Thank you.

51:08.520 --> 51:10.800
Now, the reason human populations

51:10.800 --> 51:13.600
have neutralizing antibodies is largely

51:13.600 --> 51:16.960
because we have exposure to natural AV capsids

51:16.960 --> 51:19.360
throughout life, but also because humans

51:19.360 --> 51:24.240
have co-evolved with capsids of primate origin.

51:24.240 --> 51:28.360
And as you guys know, every single capsid

51:28.360 --> 51:31.960
we have in a clinical trial is either of primate origin

51:31.960 --> 51:34.800
or has been engineered from primate origin.

51:34.800 --> 51:36.080
So it's a massive problem.

51:38.120 --> 51:40.960
We hypothesized that if we could find capsids

51:40.960 --> 51:43.280
from a non-primate origin,

51:43.280 --> 51:45.080
we'd be able to get around this problem.

51:45.080 --> 51:47.240
Now, we're not the first ones to do it.

51:47.240 --> 51:51.200
There are landmark papers that have done it before.

51:51.200 --> 51:53.560
But one of the things that we thought

51:53.560 --> 51:57.640
was relatively unexplored is the heterogenomic space

51:57.640 --> 51:59.280
or the extra genomic space.

52:00.440 --> 52:02.840
So to aid discovery from that,

52:02.880 --> 52:06.680
we build this custom-built capsid discovery pipeline

52:06.680 --> 52:08.120
called MATCAP.

52:08.120 --> 52:11.680
I know you've up to your neck in acronyms, this is ours.

52:13.320 --> 52:16.880
The challenge with finding or mining metagenomes,

52:17.720 --> 52:20.920
capsids from metagenomes is just the scale

52:20.920 --> 52:21.920
at which you have to find.

52:21.920 --> 52:25.360
It's literally a proverbial needle in a haystack.

52:25.360 --> 52:28.760
We have about four, if you just go to NCBI,

52:28.760 --> 52:33.480
you have about 4.2 million SRAs or Sequence Read Archives

52:33.480 --> 52:34.880
that you have to search through,

52:34.880 --> 52:36.760
and that represents petabytes of data.

52:36.760 --> 52:39.760
So we built this three-layer screen

52:39.760 --> 52:42.880
where we first did a more crude screen

52:42.880 --> 52:46.040
to just identify SRAs that had power viral reads.

52:46.040 --> 52:49.720
So out of these 4.2 million, we landed about 12,000 of them.

52:49.720 --> 52:54.720
And then we essentially assembled these contexts de novo

52:54.880 --> 52:58.800
by overlapping these reads through Synoteca II,

52:58.800 --> 53:00.880
and then these contexts were annotated,

53:00.880 --> 53:03.720
and we identified novel AAV capsids

53:03.720 --> 53:05.640
from that batch of things.

53:08.840 --> 53:11.720
Using this method, we found capsids throughout the globe.

53:11.720 --> 53:14.760
We found a lot of capsids from the US,

53:14.760 --> 53:17.440
which is not surprising because there is a lot of studies

53:17.440 --> 53:20.160
in metagenomic studies in wastewater from the US,

53:20.160 --> 53:23.840
but we also found a lot of capsids in Southeast Asia.

53:25.160 --> 53:28.880
We found capsids in a variety of architectures.

53:28.880 --> 53:30.120
We found linear capsids.

53:30.120 --> 53:32.960
We found in complete genomes,

53:32.960 --> 53:34.920
with just the capsidum and no rep.

53:34.920 --> 53:38.600
We found capsids that looked like they could be circular

53:38.600 --> 53:41.960
in their genomes, not entirely clear why.

53:41.960 --> 53:46.960
But as a summation, we found about 139 novel capsids.

53:48.000 --> 53:49.280
So just to set the scale,

53:49.280 --> 53:54.280
this about doubles the universe of known AAV capsidum.

53:55.280 --> 53:58.280
So that's what we found in this class of variants.

53:58.280 --> 54:01.440
What is particularly interesting in this cladogram

54:01.440 --> 54:06.440
that you see is the divergent madcap sequence.

54:06.440 --> 54:08.440
So divergent madcap sequence is a sequence

54:08.440 --> 54:13.440
that we actually could not find a close natural relative to.

54:13.440 --> 54:17.040
There wasn't really a base capsid or a parental capsid

54:17.040 --> 54:19.840
that we could see that it had evolved away from.

54:19.840 --> 54:23.680
And that's what we're gonna talk about throughout this talk,

54:23.720 --> 54:27.040
and focus on, given the context of what we were searching for.

54:29.440 --> 54:33.400
In this data set, we are looking at percentage neutralization.

54:33.400 --> 54:37.720
And I like to point you out sort of the two big buckets

54:37.720 --> 54:39.200
of capsids that we were able to find.

54:39.200 --> 54:40.920
There's this blue bucket, right?

54:40.920 --> 54:42.440
Which is mildly neutralizable.

54:42.440 --> 54:45.200
They get neutralized, especially as you keep increasing

54:45.200 --> 54:47.200
the concentration of human IVIG,

54:47.200 --> 54:49.360
which contains preexisting neutralizing antibodies

54:49.360 --> 54:51.240
throughout the globe.

54:51.280 --> 54:54.080
They are much better compared to the black lines there,

54:54.080 --> 54:58.440
which are the natural isolates like AV1, AV2, and AV9.

54:58.440 --> 55:01.440
And then you have what we think is the most promising one,

55:01.440 --> 55:04.760
which are the pink squiggly lines, the pink capsids here,

55:04.760 --> 55:07.160
which are these non-neutralizing capsids

55:07.160 --> 55:09.760
that do not seem to hit 50% neutralization

55:09.760 --> 55:12.480
at any concentration of IVIG that we dose.

55:12.480 --> 55:17.400
There's literally no nab response

55:17.400 --> 55:20.000
that we could record for these, which is very surprising.

55:20.280 --> 55:23.960
We have not seen that previously.

55:23.960 --> 55:27.360
This is just the same data graphed out differently

55:27.360 --> 55:30.560
to show you the gulf between the natural isolates,

55:30.560 --> 55:32.840
the neutralizable mat caps,

55:32.840 --> 55:35.400
and the completely non-neutralizable mat caps.

55:36.960 --> 55:40.800
We decided to focus on two of these pink capsids,

55:40.800 --> 55:44.840
two of the non-neutralizable mat caps, MC46 and MC55.

55:44.840 --> 55:46.280
Again, more acronyms.

55:47.120 --> 55:49.920
I'll talk to you about in a second

55:49.920 --> 55:52.040
of why we decided to focus on it,

55:52.040 --> 55:53.400
but the first thing to show is that

55:53.400 --> 55:57.160
because these are caps as we haven't seen before,

55:57.160 --> 55:59.080
we confirm that these are able to package,

55:59.080 --> 56:02.520
these are able to make what we consider classic virions.

56:03.520 --> 56:05.800
But very interestingly, when we dose them

56:05.800 --> 56:10.240
into a non-human primate, these stood out significantly

56:10.240 --> 56:11.480
compared to AV9.

56:11.480 --> 56:15.000
You can see AV9 on the bottom right of the curve,

56:15.000 --> 56:18.840
high neutralization, but low brain penetrance,

56:18.840 --> 56:22.280
and you can see MC55, and especially MC46,

56:22.280 --> 56:24.280
on the left of that curve.

56:26.160 --> 56:29.240
If you look at where they are on the cladogram

56:31.200 --> 56:33.760
in relation to other capsids,

56:33.760 --> 56:37.880
what you're seeing here in the purple Manhattan skyscrapers

56:37.880 --> 56:41.280
is the extent to which they can be neutralized.

56:41.280 --> 56:43.840
And you can clearly point out with those two boxes

56:43.840 --> 56:48.200
that MC45, MC46 is completely non-neutralizable,

56:48.200 --> 56:51.160
whereas MC55 has a degree of neutralization,

56:51.160 --> 56:53.280
but is much less compared to its neighbors

56:53.280 --> 56:55.480
and natural isolates.

56:57.360 --> 56:59.720
Here is a representative brain map

56:59.720 --> 57:03.840
showing the brain penetrance of MC46 and MC55

57:03.840 --> 57:05.720
compared to AV9.

57:05.720 --> 57:08.880
What you're seeing on the left is DNA fitness,

57:08.880 --> 57:10.280
or DNA recounts.

57:10.280 --> 57:12.960
What you're seeing on the right of this brain heat map

57:12.960 --> 57:16.560
is the RNA, and you can see that we think that MC46

57:16.560 --> 57:19.240
and MC55 show widespread transduction

57:19.240 --> 57:22.480
of the non-human primate brain after intravenous delivery.

57:25.200 --> 57:29.680
We wanted to next confirm how good,

57:29.680 --> 57:34.280
or how good at avoiding nab neutralization are these capsids.

57:34.280 --> 57:35.800
So we basically compared them

57:35.800 --> 57:37.880
to their closest natural isolate.

57:37.880 --> 57:40.000
I'm just showing you this MSA just to show you

57:40.000 --> 57:42.800
that when we say closest is not very close,

57:42.800 --> 57:44.280
it's actually quite far,

57:44.280 --> 57:46.280
but their closest natural isolates,

57:46.280 --> 57:49.280
and we benchmarked it to AV1 and AV9.

57:50.120 --> 57:53.040
And what you're seeing here is basically

57:53.040 --> 57:55.680
person neutralization in about 15

57:55.680 --> 57:58.120
individual human donors that we sourced.

57:58.120 --> 58:00.840
And what you can see here, the more yellier you get,

58:00.840 --> 58:04.440
the more seropositive these donors are

58:04.440 --> 58:07.240
for a specific capsid.

58:07.240 --> 58:10.280
And what you can see is that your natural AV capsids,

58:10.280 --> 58:13.080
a majority of donors are seropositive for them.

58:13.080 --> 58:17.320
As you go to MC55, which is the last but one column,

58:17.320 --> 58:20.520
you can see that few donors are seropositive.

58:20.520 --> 58:21.880
And as you get to MC46,

58:21.880 --> 58:24.360
we did not see a single donor out of the 15

58:24.360 --> 58:26.000
that was seropositive.

58:26.000 --> 58:27.440
We've actually done this exact study

58:27.440 --> 58:29.920
with about 100 non-human primates right now,

58:29.920 --> 58:33.320
and we found two that maybe could be seropositive.

58:35.320 --> 58:39.340
We also confirmed that if you take serum from these donors,

58:39.340 --> 58:42.740
you are still able to get full transduction

58:42.740 --> 58:45.300
with MC46 and MC55.

58:48.380 --> 58:51.140
Now the next question is, if that is true,

58:52.100 --> 58:54.780
is this a paradigm for re-dosing?

58:55.700 --> 58:58.320
What we confirmed is that MC46 transduction

58:58.320 --> 59:00.180
is unaffected by prior AV dosing.

59:00.180 --> 59:02.700
So on the left, we are showing you

59:02.700 --> 59:04.420
this passive transfer model,

59:04.420 --> 59:07.140
where rodents are pre-dosed with human IVIG,

59:07.140 --> 59:09.300
and then they are challenged with AV.

59:09.300 --> 59:11.380
And what you can see is that as expected,

59:11.380 --> 59:14.620
AV9 completely loses all transduction

59:14.620 --> 59:19.100
if in a passive transfer mouse model,

59:19.100 --> 59:22.540
which is rescued by IDES, as expected.

59:22.540 --> 59:26.480
On the other hand, MC46 loses none of its transduction.

59:30.020 --> 59:32.460
And a pretty similar assay on the right,

59:32.460 --> 59:34.620
where we are showing that in rodents

59:34.620 --> 59:37.620
that are dosed with AV1, or AVRS7233,

59:37.620 --> 59:39.100
pretty diverse capsules,

59:39.100 --> 59:40.980
you see exactly the same phenomena

59:40.980 --> 59:43.100
where gene transfer is not inhibited.

59:44.260 --> 59:47.300
Using this when challenged with the seed of these mice,

59:47.300 --> 59:49.660
there is no inhibition of gene transfer at all

59:49.660 --> 59:51.540
with MC46 and MC55.

59:54.180 --> 59:58.020
So your holy grail capsid

59:58.020 --> 01:00:02.180
has all three of these components augmented.

01:00:03.020 --> 01:00:05.980
Last year, we showed you some data at ASGCT on Apex,

01:00:05.980 --> 01:00:08.020
which is our generative AI modeling,

01:00:08.020 --> 01:00:11.100
where we take a known capsid, a known natural isolate,

01:00:11.100 --> 01:00:13.220
and we evolve the sequence space around it

01:00:13.220 --> 01:00:15.100
with generative AI modeling

01:00:15.100 --> 01:00:17.700
to find a retinotropic capsid

01:00:17.700 --> 01:00:19.220
that is both manufacturable

01:00:19.220 --> 01:00:22.220
and has high degree of retinotropism.

01:00:23.060 --> 01:00:24.540
This year, we're talking about mad gas,

01:00:24.540 --> 01:00:26.700
where instead of going this way,

01:00:26.700 --> 01:00:28.140
we're going this way,

01:00:28.140 --> 01:00:30.580
and discovering novel ABs by mining

01:00:30.580 --> 01:00:33.580
this vast amount of available metagenomic space.

01:00:35.980 --> 01:00:37.740
Why are we excited about this work?

01:00:37.780 --> 01:00:39.940
We are excited about this work

01:00:39.940 --> 01:00:41.980
because of the discovery potential, right?

01:00:41.980 --> 01:00:43.260
So we have hit upon this,

01:00:43.260 --> 01:00:47.900
what we think is this incredibly black box unexplored

01:00:47.900 --> 01:00:52.180
mother lord of this rich vein of nav-naive capsids

01:00:52.180 --> 01:00:55.220
that have exciting properties that we don't know about.

01:00:56.540 --> 01:01:00.020
We are excited to find out more about the biology of this.

01:01:00.020 --> 01:01:01.780
Why do these capsids,

01:01:01.780 --> 01:01:06.540
how these capsids evolve to avoid nab recognition?

01:01:06.540 --> 01:01:11.020
How is MC-46 doing what it's doing

01:01:11.020 --> 01:01:12.660
in the non-human primate CNS,

01:01:12.660 --> 01:01:15.780
and why it hasn't likely seen a primate?

01:01:17.620 --> 01:01:20.340
Is it, does it have deep brain penetrance,

01:01:20.340 --> 01:01:22.580
or is it limited to basically existing

01:01:22.580 --> 01:01:24.460
or transducing the blood-brain barrier?

01:01:24.460 --> 01:01:26.340
Does it actually cross it?

01:01:26.340 --> 01:01:29.500
Are these novel capsids responsive to AVR,

01:01:29.500 --> 01:01:31.780
or is there a different pathway by which they go through?

01:01:31.780 --> 01:01:34.980
What is the natural receptor for these capsids?

01:01:34.980 --> 01:01:38.500
So much, so much interesting biology that we are,

01:01:38.500 --> 01:01:41.380
we are digging into and every day is an exciting day.

01:01:42.780 --> 01:01:46.460
We're excited for the impact that it can have on patients.

01:01:46.460 --> 01:01:51.460
Very clearly, this satisfies a very fundamental ask

01:01:51.940 --> 01:01:53.460
from the capsid engineering community.

01:01:53.460 --> 01:01:57.220
Can you give us a capsule that is completely nab naive?

01:01:57.220 --> 01:02:00.020
And we are already working with some teams

01:02:00.020 --> 01:02:02.820
on exploring this in the pre-clinical space.

01:02:02.940 --> 01:02:06.580
If this is of interest to any of you,

01:02:06.580 --> 01:02:08.700
please feel free to reach out,

01:02:08.700 --> 01:02:10.340
and we'll have a chat on this.

01:02:10.340 --> 01:02:12.420
We are completely open about sharing these.

01:02:12.420 --> 01:02:14.140
We are completely, we really want people

01:02:14.140 --> 01:02:16.180
to be using it if it's of interest.

01:02:17.100 --> 01:02:21.260
And lastly, speaking as a capsid biologist,

01:02:22.340 --> 01:02:24.940
I love this for the platform value that it brings.

01:02:24.940 --> 01:02:29.140
Like it's, the value for us is now this is a chassis for us

01:02:29.140 --> 01:02:32.420
to evolve capsid with desired properties based off on.

01:02:32.420 --> 01:02:36.460
We've essentially switched from your AV9 and like

01:02:36.460 --> 01:02:40.220
to these matcap capsids for our chassis almost immediately

01:02:40.220 --> 01:02:43.100
because I think this is how we're gonna,

01:02:43.100 --> 01:02:46.100
our lab or our team is gonna start doing capsid discovery.

01:02:47.340 --> 01:02:50.900
So it takes a village, it's not just us.

01:02:50.900 --> 01:02:53.460
I like to highlight three individuals,

01:02:53.460 --> 01:02:57.020
Tess Tarragosa, Eugenia Lashanko and Alex Isasi,

01:02:57.020 --> 01:02:58.900
who are the key contributors for this

01:02:58.900 --> 01:03:00.940
and are the first authors in a manuscript

01:03:00.940 --> 01:03:03.300
that is under review.

01:03:03.300 --> 01:03:06.060
And I'd like to have a heartfelt thanks

01:03:06.060 --> 01:03:10.460
for Christian Mueller, who's the global head of JMU,

01:03:10.460 --> 01:03:13.500
for investing time and resource into something,

01:03:13.500 --> 01:03:15.620
especially in the early days when it's blue sky research.

01:03:15.620 --> 01:03:18.940
It's sometimes hard to do that with corporate KPIs

01:03:18.940 --> 01:03:20.860
and term sheets and all of that.

01:03:20.860 --> 01:03:24.540
So with that, thank you, and I'm happy to take questions.

01:03:24.540 --> 01:03:25.380
Thank you.

01:03:30.380 --> 01:03:31.940
Thank you, Sara.

01:03:31.940 --> 01:03:33.180
Hi, nice talk.

