I'm Dan Stone from Fred Hutch.

01:03:34.540 --> 01:03:36.220
I'm also the associate editor in chief

01:03:36.220 --> 01:03:37.700
of molecular therapy advances,

01:03:37.700 --> 01:03:40.340
which I'll get to why I'm saying that in a second.

01:03:41.460 --> 01:03:46.380
So many of us in the room have submitted AV sequences

01:03:46.380 --> 01:03:48.380
and capsids to SRA.

01:03:49.380 --> 01:03:51.780
So when you are panning this stuff,

01:03:51.780 --> 01:03:55.340
if say I had a novel capsid that I'd submitted the SRA to

01:03:55.340 --> 01:03:57.180
and I hadn't published it,

01:03:57.180 --> 01:04:01.740
would that be captured in your novel AV

01:04:01.740 --> 01:04:02.580
that you're pulling out?

01:04:02.580 --> 01:04:04.180
So really, are you discovering things

01:04:04.180 --> 01:04:06.540
that people have other discovered, already discovered,

01:04:06.540 --> 01:04:09.020
or is this stuff that's coming from, say,

01:04:09.020 --> 01:04:12.580
a non-AV related study that happens to have an AV in there?

01:04:12.580 --> 01:04:13.980
The part one.

01:04:13.980 --> 01:04:15.580
Okay.

01:04:15.580 --> 01:04:16.940
The latter.

01:04:16.940 --> 01:04:19.380
In fact, I can tell you that MC46 comes

01:04:19.380 --> 01:04:23.620
from a completely unrelated study, nothing to do with AV,

01:04:23.620 --> 01:04:27.300
generally more to do with a post-COVID virology screen

01:04:27.300 --> 01:04:29.700
in a seafood market in Southeast Asia.

01:04:29.700 --> 01:04:31.180
So nothing to do with AV.

01:04:31.180 --> 01:04:32.020
Okay, great.

01:04:32.020 --> 01:04:32.860
Thank you.

01:04:32.860 --> 01:04:33.820
And then part two.

01:04:33.820 --> 01:04:35.420
So at molecular therapy advances,

01:04:35.420 --> 01:04:37.900
we publish a lot of novel AV capsids

01:04:37.900 --> 01:04:42.900
and we see a lot of biotechs wanting to publish capsids,

01:04:43.580 --> 01:04:46.980
but they call them AVXB, et cetera.

01:04:47.020 --> 01:04:50.020
Since you are harvesting this from publicly available database,

01:04:50.020 --> 01:04:52.860
are you willing to put these capsid sequences

01:04:52.860 --> 01:04:55.500
back into the databases where you got them from?

01:04:55.500 --> 01:04:56.340
Absolutely.

01:04:58.020 --> 01:05:01.020
I speak for Sanofi here that we are incredibly open.

01:05:01.020 --> 01:05:05.900
We've published Apex and other platforms we've published.

01:05:05.900 --> 01:05:07.980
This is, as I said, in press.

01:05:07.980 --> 01:05:09.540
We will absolutely have a full disclosure,

01:05:09.540 --> 01:05:12.820
including not just the sequences, which is obvious,

01:05:12.820 --> 01:05:14.900
but obviously also the code by which you discovered it,

01:05:14.900 --> 01:05:16.540
in case there are things that we didn't discover,

01:05:16.540 --> 01:05:17.820
that other people find interesting.

01:05:17.820 --> 01:05:19.500
So we are entirely transparent.

01:05:19.500 --> 01:05:21.460
That's fantastic, and I commend you

01:05:21.460 --> 01:05:23.700
and I recommend that all other companies

01:05:23.700 --> 01:05:25.740
that submit to any of the molecular therapy journals

01:05:25.740 --> 01:05:27.580
have a similar...

01:05:27.580 --> 01:05:28.420
I love it.

01:05:28.420 --> 01:05:29.260
Thank you very much.

01:05:29.260 --> 01:05:30.100
Thank you.

01:05:32.180 --> 01:05:34.260
Hi, great talk.

01:05:34.260 --> 01:05:36.180
Ashuna from University of Louisville.

01:05:36.180 --> 01:05:38.420
So anything that we make novel

01:05:38.420 --> 01:05:42.420
also can trigger the immunogenicity profile.

01:05:42.420 --> 01:05:44.500
Have you looked into how this,

01:05:45.300 --> 01:05:46.900
while the transduction is great,

01:05:46.900 --> 01:05:49.780
but how does it trigger the innate

01:05:49.780 --> 01:05:52.740
and the innate immunity of these?

01:05:52.740 --> 01:05:55.100
Have you looked into the immunity profiles

01:05:55.100 --> 01:05:58.900
of these animals that you have been investigating?

01:05:58.900 --> 01:05:59.740
Great question.

01:05:59.740 --> 01:06:00.740
We do.

01:06:00.740 --> 01:06:02.460
We have not seen anything adverse.

01:06:02.460 --> 01:06:04.460
The animals, the non-human primates,

01:06:04.460 --> 01:06:07.100
seem to be able to tolerate it

01:06:07.100 --> 01:06:09.580
with the specific set of trans genes with a caveat

01:06:09.580 --> 01:06:11.980
that we have a specific set of trans genes

01:06:11.980 --> 01:06:13.980
that we are interested in and so on and so forth.

01:06:13.980 --> 01:06:16.660
With that caveat, we have seen no adverse effects.

01:06:16.660 --> 01:06:18.580
We've seen some very interesting phenomena

01:06:18.580 --> 01:06:21.180
in terms of, which we still have to confirm

01:06:21.180 --> 01:06:25.100
in terms of it seems, whether it can be completely

01:06:25.100 --> 01:06:28.100
re-dosed as a second dose by itself.

01:06:28.100 --> 01:06:29.860
I think that study is ongoing,

01:06:29.860 --> 01:06:32.780
but in terms of phenomena that might be interesting

01:06:32.780 --> 01:06:34.940
to the clinical community, these behave

01:06:34.940 --> 01:06:37.380
pretty much like other capsules.

01:06:37.380 --> 01:06:38.380
Okay, thank you.

01:06:40.340 --> 01:06:43.740
In the interest of time, maybe we can have the questions

01:06:43.740 --> 01:06:44.580
later.

01:06:44.580 --> 01:06:47.380
I see there are many more questions for you.

01:06:47.380 --> 01:06:50.180
So maybe you can talk to all the people.

01:06:50.180 --> 01:06:51.020
I would love to.

01:06:51.020 --> 01:06:53.220
Please, please, please feel free to catch me.

01:06:53.220 --> 01:06:54.380
Thank you, Saurabh.

01:06:54.380 --> 01:06:55.220
Thank you.

01:06:55.220 --> 01:06:56.060
Thank you.

01:06:56.060 --> 01:06:56.900
Thank you.

01:06:59.900 --> 01:07:02.300
Moving on to our next speaker.

01:07:02.300 --> 01:07:06.660
And I want to emphasize here that he is a travel awardee.

01:07:06.660 --> 01:07:11.540
And here we welcome John Zachary for his talk

01:07:11.580 --> 01:07:13.500
on the structural investigation

01:07:13.500 --> 01:07:17.860
of naturally occurring human anti-AV2 antibodies

01:07:17.860 --> 01:07:21.180
with antigenic profile of AV2.

01:07:21.180 --> 01:07:22.300
And welcome, John.

01:07:22.300 --> 01:07:23.700
Hi, good morning, thank you.

01:07:23.700 --> 01:07:26.500
All right, so as she said, my name's John,

01:07:26.500 --> 01:07:29.540
and I am a PhD student at the University of Florida,

01:07:29.540 --> 01:07:32.100
so I don't have any disclosures at this time.

01:07:32.100 --> 01:07:34.180
I'm gonna go ahead and launch into a portion

01:07:34.180 --> 01:07:38.780
of my dissertation work that we're working on currently.

01:07:38.780 --> 01:07:43.780
So as we just heard, the use of AAVs in the clinic

01:07:43.980 --> 01:07:47.300
uses a large variety of native occurring capsids,

01:07:47.300 --> 01:07:49.100
and those capsids have preexisting,

01:07:49.100 --> 01:07:50.740
neutralizing antibodies circulating

01:07:50.740 --> 01:07:52.260
throughout the population.

01:07:52.260 --> 01:07:54.660
In fact, as of last January,

01:07:54.660 --> 01:07:57.740
about 80% of the clinical trials that had occurred

01:07:57.740 --> 01:08:01.540
or were occurring at the time used native type capsids.

01:08:01.540 --> 01:08:04.020
And as we've seen in the past few days,

01:08:04.020 --> 01:08:06.300
the proportion of engineered capsids

01:08:06.300 --> 01:08:07.980
can be expected to increase,

01:08:07.980 --> 01:08:12.260
but the resistance through neutralizing antibodies

01:08:12.260 --> 01:08:14.540
may not be affected by some of these changes.

01:08:14.540 --> 01:08:16.780
And depending on the serotype that you investigate,

01:08:16.780 --> 01:08:20.780
you can have anywhere from five to 95% seropositivity

01:08:20.780 --> 01:08:23.460
for this neutralizing antibodies in your population.

01:08:24.660 --> 01:08:27.140
In response to this, our lab recently,

01:08:27.140 --> 01:08:31.940
and a few years ago, did a study using AV9 antibodies

01:08:31.940 --> 01:08:35.220
that were sourced from Zoologism treated patients

01:08:35.220 --> 01:08:36.980
that were neutralizing.

01:08:36.980 --> 01:08:41.620
And then use high resolution cryoem data

01:08:41.620 --> 01:08:45.900
in order to characterize where those exact antibodies

01:08:45.900 --> 01:08:47.260
bound on the surface.

01:08:47.260 --> 01:08:50.980
And from that, we determined about three quarters

01:08:50.980 --> 01:08:52.060
of them bound to the surface

01:08:52.060 --> 01:08:53.620
at the two full portion of the capsid.

01:08:53.620 --> 01:08:55.140
And for those of you that aren't overly familiar

01:08:55.140 --> 01:08:58.180
with the exact structure, I'll show that in a moment.

01:08:59.540 --> 01:09:00.780
And then from this data,

01:09:00.780 --> 01:09:04.100
we then developed a antibody escape capable variant

01:09:04.100 --> 01:09:06.820
that was able to escape 17 of those 21 antibodies

01:09:06.820 --> 01:09:09.540
that we investigated, maintained its tropism

01:09:09.540 --> 01:09:12.300
and biodistributions and maintained transduction

01:09:12.300 --> 01:09:14.460
in the presence of sera, both from these patients

01:09:14.460 --> 01:09:16.420
that these antibodies were isolated from

01:09:16.420 --> 01:09:18.860
and from patients whose antibodies weren't included

01:09:18.860 --> 01:09:20.340
in this portion of the study.

01:09:22.340 --> 01:09:26.100
Recently, there's been a, well, in 2022,

01:09:26.100 --> 01:09:27.780
following the COVID lockdown,

01:09:27.780 --> 01:09:29.900
there was an outbreak of pediatric hepatitis

01:09:29.900 --> 01:09:31.620
and during the ideological studies for these,

01:09:31.620 --> 01:09:35.220
patients were found to also be co-infected with AAV2.

01:09:35.220 --> 01:09:39.740
This was not necessarily unexpected as many of the viruses

01:09:39.740 --> 01:09:42.660
that were found to be part of this outbreak

01:09:42.660 --> 01:09:46.460
were the common helper viruses for AAVs.

01:09:46.460 --> 01:09:49.140
And using access to our collaborators,

01:09:49.140 --> 01:09:52.420
we were able to generate samples of the antibodies

01:09:52.420 --> 01:09:54.500
from these patients that are specific

01:09:54.500 --> 01:09:58.860
to this native type infection for AAV2.

01:09:58.860 --> 01:10:01.620
And so the goal of this work then is to clone out those

01:10:01.620 --> 01:10:05.200
and generate a high resolution cryoAM data again,

01:10:05.200 --> 01:10:07.640
in order to identify those specific epitopes

01:10:07.640 --> 01:10:10.680
on the surface of the capsid and compare that for now

01:10:10.680 --> 01:10:13.000
to the epitopes found in AAV9

01:10:13.000 --> 01:10:16.080
and other mouse-derived anti-AAV2 antibodies.

01:10:17.640 --> 01:10:19.880
And so beginning with the work from my collaborators,

01:10:19.880 --> 01:10:21.360
they were able to isolate out these,

01:10:21.360 --> 01:10:24.240
demonstrate both binding and neutralization in vitro,

01:10:24.240 --> 01:10:26.120
which is important for this type of work.

01:10:26.120 --> 01:10:30.320
And moving on from there, using high resolution cryoAM,

01:10:30.320 --> 01:10:33.160
I've characterized the binding of 10 of these so far.

01:10:33.280 --> 01:10:35.920
Go ahead and break that down for you.

01:10:35.920 --> 01:10:38.000
The AAV capsid is an icosahedron,

01:10:38.000 --> 01:10:39.720
and so it has a two-fold, three-fold,

01:10:39.720 --> 01:10:41.360
and five-fold symmetry axes,

01:10:41.360 --> 01:10:45.280
and these give rise to different important

01:10:45.280 --> 01:10:47.760
structural features on the surface of the capsid.

01:10:47.760 --> 01:10:50.880
And for the clusters of antibodies that I have,

01:10:50.880 --> 01:10:54.760
five of these AAV2 antibodies bound around the three-fold,

01:10:54.760 --> 01:10:57.320
which is about 50% of these,

01:10:57.320 --> 01:11:00.920
two bound to the two-fold and three bound to the five-fold.

01:11:00.920 --> 01:11:03.360
And then doing the same breakdown for AAV9,

01:11:03.360 --> 01:11:05.640
just to remind us, as I said,

01:11:05.640 --> 01:11:08.960
three-quarters of these 21 bound to the two-fold,

01:11:08.960 --> 01:11:12.160
so that's about 16, if I remember correctly,

01:11:12.160 --> 01:11:14.160
and then two bound to the three-fold

01:11:14.160 --> 01:11:15.960
and three bound to the five-fold.

01:11:15.960 --> 01:11:18.760
Now, this is a very interesting piece of information,

01:11:18.760 --> 01:11:22.040
but structurally, why is this happening?

01:11:22.040 --> 01:11:22.880
And so,

01:11:25.640 --> 01:11:28.760
I must have pressed it back at some point, sorry.

01:11:28.760 --> 01:11:30.720
All right, here we go, next slide.

01:11:30.720 --> 01:11:33.320
All right, so just to re-orient you for a moment,

01:11:33.320 --> 01:11:35.480
now we're looking down at a section of the capsid

01:11:35.480 --> 01:11:38.200
that have been pulled out and look isolated,

01:11:38.200 --> 01:11:39.480
and so the three-folds there,

01:11:39.480 --> 01:11:40.640
represented by the triangles,

01:11:40.640 --> 01:11:42.640
and the two-fold by this oval shape,

01:11:42.640 --> 01:11:44.560
and I wanna draw your attention first

01:11:44.560 --> 01:11:46.920
to these red squares on the top.

01:11:46.920 --> 01:11:48.600
The top portions of the panel here

01:11:48.600 --> 01:11:52.760
are hydrophobicity matrix six on the surface of the capsid,

01:11:52.760 --> 01:11:55.600
and those two squares on the right are highlighting

01:11:55.600 --> 01:11:59.080
two residues that are found within the two-fold pocket

01:11:59.080 --> 01:12:01.200
on the surface of the capsid,

01:12:01.200 --> 01:12:05.600
and these residues were bound by 100%

01:12:05.600 --> 01:12:07.040
for the one on the furthest right,

01:12:07.040 --> 01:12:10.640
and 50% of the antibodies that bounded the two-fold.

01:12:10.640 --> 01:12:12.960
And then, looking onto the left box,

01:12:12.960 --> 01:12:15.240
we see that there's this residue here,

01:12:15.240 --> 01:12:16.520
this is a tryptophan that's found

01:12:16.520 --> 01:12:19.040
in the center of the three-fold protrusions here,

01:12:19.040 --> 01:12:20.200
and this was bound by both

01:12:20.200 --> 01:12:22.600
of those three-fold binding antibodies.

01:12:22.600 --> 01:12:24.960
Conversely, when we look at AAV2,

01:12:24.960 --> 01:12:27.600
you can see that those hydrophobic spots

01:12:27.600 --> 01:12:30.520
were represented by those orange colors are not present,

01:12:30.520 --> 01:12:35.120
and AAV2, that right box has been changed

01:12:35.120 --> 01:12:37.200
from a tyrosine to an asparagine,

01:12:37.200 --> 01:12:39.760
no longer has this hydrophobic interaction surface,

01:12:39.760 --> 01:12:42.120
and then the surface of the AAV2 capsid,

01:12:42.120 --> 01:12:43.960
actually, at the three-fold protrusion,

01:12:43.960 --> 01:12:46.120
extends out over the two-fold pocket

01:12:46.120 --> 01:12:49.440
and presents itself and obscures the interaction

01:12:49.440 --> 01:12:52.960
that would occur to the, oh, there's another one here.

01:12:52.960 --> 01:12:54.400
That would obscures the interaction

01:12:54.400 --> 01:12:56.800
to that other residue,

01:12:56.800 --> 01:13:00.960
and the tryptophan also is not present in the AAV2 surface.

01:13:00.960 --> 01:13:02.720
At the bottom panel now, we're looking

01:13:02.720 --> 01:13:06.560
at an electrostatic representation of the capsid surface.

01:13:06.560 --> 01:13:08.760
I'm gonna draw your attention to the pink squares.

01:13:08.760 --> 01:13:10.120
Good.

01:13:10.120 --> 01:13:13.000
And this pocket highlighted on the right for the AAV9,

01:13:13.000 --> 01:13:16.200
it's an intensely negatively charged portion of the capsid,

01:13:16.200 --> 01:13:20.040
which, in general, antibodies tend to favor,

01:13:20.040 --> 01:13:25.040
though it's not entirely 100% of the time,

01:13:25.120 --> 01:13:27.480
negative regions on antigenic surfaces,

01:13:27.480 --> 01:13:30.440
and this pocket is obscured in AAV2

01:13:30.440 --> 01:13:32.240
when we look at the left pink box.

01:13:32.240 --> 01:13:33.920
I wanna draw your attention to the greater region

01:13:33.920 --> 01:13:37.480
of the AAV2 surface, which highlights,

01:13:37.480 --> 01:13:39.280
trying to highlight that there's just, in general,

01:13:39.280 --> 01:13:40.960
a larger distribution of charges

01:13:40.960 --> 01:13:43.880
across the AAV2 surface outside of that pocket,

01:13:43.880 --> 01:13:47.280
so there's easier points to contact for AAV2

01:13:47.280 --> 01:13:50.240
versus that deep pocket for AAV9.

01:13:50.240 --> 01:13:53.520
And then when we make this a similar comparison

01:13:53.520 --> 01:13:55.720
to mouse monoclonal antibodies

01:13:55.720 --> 01:13:59.320
that were generated towards AAV2 in the past,

01:13:59.320 --> 01:14:02.400
there are two so far that I wanna point out here.

01:14:02.400 --> 01:14:05.240
We have Fab A20, which is a five-fold binder,

01:14:05.240 --> 01:14:08.280
and Fab C37B, which is a three-fold binder,

01:14:08.280 --> 01:14:12.760
and when we look at a stereographic representation

01:14:12.760 --> 01:14:15.480
of the binding footprints of these antibodies,

01:14:15.480 --> 01:14:19.520
we see that in the case of anti-AAV2 mouse antibodies,

01:14:19.520 --> 01:14:21.840
they're fairly prototypic of what we see for humans,

01:14:21.840 --> 01:14:24.560
and this is different from what we saw for AAV9,

01:14:24.560 --> 01:14:27.520
where anti-AAV9 antibodies for mice

01:14:27.520 --> 01:14:32.520
were not highly similar to human antibodies for AAV9.

01:14:33.840 --> 01:14:38.040
This is true for all except for the two two-fold binders,

01:14:38.040 --> 01:14:41.200
which for now there's not a mouse monoclonal antibody,

01:14:41.200 --> 01:14:42.680
it's representative of these,

01:14:42.680 --> 01:14:45.960
but in the past, work has been used A20

01:14:45.960 --> 01:14:50.080
as a prototypic escape for human IVIG,

01:14:50.080 --> 01:14:52.640
and so this kind of highlights some of the structural

01:14:54.000 --> 01:14:58.560
underlying reasons for that capability.

01:14:58.560 --> 01:15:00.880
Moving forward, we're taking this on

01:15:00.880 --> 01:15:04.640
to develop an escape variant as we did in the past,

01:15:04.640 --> 01:15:08.200
and I'm working on that currently.

01:15:08.200 --> 01:15:10.000
So just as a summary,

01:15:10.000 --> 01:15:12.880
talking about the antigenic profile of AAV2

01:15:12.880 --> 01:15:17.600
seems to favor the three-fold a bit more than for AAV9,

01:15:17.600 --> 01:15:19.880
and while this is one of our studies,

01:15:20.520 --> 01:15:22.800
there are fairly few studies that fully characterize

01:15:22.800 --> 01:15:25.800
the antigenic regions of AAVs in general,

01:15:25.800 --> 01:15:28.120
and this represents right now the largest study

01:15:28.120 --> 01:15:31.440
of human anti-AAV2 antibodies,

01:15:32.680 --> 01:15:34.920
and is the only study that's conducted

01:15:34.920 --> 01:15:37.400
on naturally occurring AAV2 infections

01:15:37.400 --> 01:15:41.400
as opposed to a clinical application of a therapy,

01:15:41.400 --> 01:15:45.400
and while I've highlighted some different structural reasons

01:15:45.400 --> 01:15:48.120
for why these antibodies could interact differently

01:15:48.160 --> 01:15:51.440
at the surface, there is also the difference

01:15:51.440 --> 01:15:53.680
in the context of the infection for AAV9.

01:15:53.680 --> 01:15:55.640
Presumably, there was no helper virus present

01:15:55.640 --> 01:15:57.280
for these patients when they were treated,

01:15:57.280 --> 01:15:59.240
and they were treated at a much higher dose

01:15:59.240 --> 01:16:01.320
than they would have been if they were exposed

01:16:01.320 --> 01:16:06.000
to AAV9 naturally, such as the case for these AAV2 patients,

01:16:06.000 --> 01:16:10.040
and for the future, we're expanding this study

01:16:10.040 --> 01:16:12.440
with another patient and other set of antibodies,

01:16:12.440 --> 01:16:16.720
and then developing of an escape capable vector

01:16:16.760 --> 01:16:19.240
for these pre-existing antibodies,

01:16:19.240 --> 01:16:22.640
and I wanna thank my lab, my PI, Dr. Rob McKenna,

01:16:22.640 --> 01:16:25.400
Mario, who will be giving a talk at 3.30 today

01:16:25.400 --> 01:16:28.640
about AAV trafficking and seeing some really interesting

01:16:28.640 --> 01:16:30.640
cool data coming out of our lab,

01:16:30.640 --> 01:16:32.480
and our collaborators at the University of Sydney,

01:16:32.480 --> 01:16:35.840
especially Dr. Grant, who will be giving a talk tomorrow

01:16:35.840 --> 01:16:39.720
about more of this data concerning the AAV2 antibody

01:16:39.720 --> 01:16:42.040
and immune response from these same patients

01:16:42.040 --> 01:16:43.560
that were used for this study,

01:16:43.560 --> 01:16:46.200
but in a different context tomorrow morning.

01:16:46.240 --> 01:16:47.560
Thank you.

01:16:47.560 --> 01:16:48.400
Thank you.

01:16:48.400 --> 01:16:49.240
Thank you.

01:16:53.480 --> 01:16:54.320
Thank you, John.

01:16:55.720 --> 01:16:56.560
Questions?

01:16:58.840 --> 01:17:00.080
There's one right there.

01:17:08.080 --> 01:17:11.000
AAV2, a natural infection in presence of a helper virus

01:17:11.000 --> 01:17:14.200
in AAV9 with Zolgensma,

01:17:14.240 --> 01:17:16.280
I'm curious how these two,

01:17:16.280 --> 01:17:18.400
because they're mapping to different regions,

01:17:18.400 --> 01:17:20.720
but in most humans actually have

01:17:20.720 --> 01:17:22.440
quite cross-reactive antibodies,

01:17:22.440 --> 01:17:25.600
and I'm curious if you could talk about the differences

01:17:25.600 --> 01:17:27.480
that you might have noticed in other studies

01:17:27.480 --> 01:17:31.640
with cross-reactive antibodies in normal human's era,

01:17:31.640 --> 01:17:33.960
and how well where those map to.

01:17:33.960 --> 01:17:35.880
So in terms of cross-reactivity

01:17:35.880 --> 01:17:37.040
for the antibodies we presented,

01:17:37.040 --> 01:17:41.160
we did do, and let me see if I have it in here.

01:17:41.160 --> 01:17:43.080
I don't have the cross-reactivity studies

01:17:43.080 --> 01:17:44.600
for the AAV2 antibodies,

01:17:44.600 --> 01:17:47.280
but we did those by native.immunable, sorry.

01:17:48.120 --> 01:17:49.960
Should be right.

01:17:54.080 --> 01:17:55.520
No, it's not on here, sorry.

01:17:56.440 --> 01:17:57.960
Several of these were cross-reactive,

01:17:57.960 --> 01:18:00.160
and we found that that kind of correlated,

01:18:00.160 --> 01:18:03.360
cross-reactivity generally correlated

01:18:03.360 --> 01:18:05.120
with some of the more conserved regions of the capsid.

01:18:05.120 --> 01:18:06.600
The five-fold region especially,

01:18:06.600 --> 01:18:09.280
which is highly conserved on the surface of the capsid,

01:18:09.280 --> 01:18:11.680
tends to be more cross-reactive than others.

01:18:11.680 --> 01:18:16.680
That was true for three of these for the AAV9,

01:18:17.280 --> 01:18:20.120
possibly more, and that data is included

01:18:20.120 --> 01:18:21.920
in those papers there.

01:18:21.920 --> 01:18:25.480
And then for the AAV2, I have one's highly cross-reactive,

01:18:25.480 --> 01:18:27.760
and that one also binds to the five-fold surface.

01:18:27.760 --> 01:18:29.920
Great, thank you so much.

01:18:29.920 --> 01:18:31.040
Great talk.

01:18:31.040 --> 01:18:32.800
I may have missed this detail at the moment,

01:18:32.800 --> 01:18:34.560
so this might be a really quick question for you.

01:18:34.560 --> 01:18:36.880
Are these all neutralizing antibodies?

01:18:36.880 --> 01:18:37.760
Yes.

01:18:37.760 --> 01:18:40.000
Okay, do you have any reason to believe

01:18:40.000 --> 01:18:42.720
why there might be a different landscape

01:18:42.720 --> 01:18:44.600
in terms of whether binding for non-neutralizers

01:18:44.600 --> 01:18:46.400
versus neutralizers, or do you think this would be

01:18:46.400 --> 01:18:49.240
a common for both classes?

01:18:50.600 --> 01:18:55.600
So, I've not looked at where non-neutralizing antibodies

01:18:55.760 --> 01:18:57.520
would necessarily bind.

01:18:57.520 --> 01:19:02.520
I don't know if we have that data, at least internally.

01:19:04.760 --> 01:19:06.360
Haven't seen any in the literature,

01:19:06.360 --> 01:19:08.840
but I would imagine that for non-neutralizing antibodies,

01:19:08.880 --> 01:19:10.280
they would probably bind away

01:19:10.280 --> 01:19:13.360
from where you would expect receptor sites,

01:19:13.360 --> 01:19:18.280
and thereby are unable to inhibit transduction

01:19:18.280 --> 01:19:20.560
directly on their own, but they're still fully capable

01:19:20.560 --> 01:19:22.880
of recruiting other elements of the immune system,

01:19:22.880 --> 01:19:26.360
complement, et cetera, in order to clear out the virus.

01:19:29.080 --> 01:19:33.200
Hi, I'm Wunit from Seaver Research.

01:19:33.200 --> 01:19:34.480
Thank you for presenting this.

01:19:34.480 --> 01:19:38.480
It was very interesting, and I understood your assumption

01:19:38.520 --> 01:19:41.200
that the differences you see might be associated

01:19:41.200 --> 01:19:43.040
with the way of transduction, but have you considered

01:19:43.040 --> 01:19:47.080
that difference in post-translational modifications

01:19:47.080 --> 01:19:49.600
as part of the AAV manufacturing process

01:19:49.600 --> 01:19:52.200
may contribute to these differences?

01:19:52.200 --> 01:19:55.840
That is an interesting question, and I haven't looked into it,

01:19:55.840 --> 01:19:58.800
but it's possible.

01:19:58.800 --> 01:20:00.600
I don't really know, have the knowledge

01:20:00.600 --> 01:20:02.080
to say much more right now.

01:20:02.080 --> 01:20:04.080
So, there are some de-amidation sites

01:20:04.080 --> 01:20:06.840
that have been shown to increase immunogenicity,

01:20:06.840 --> 01:20:09.680
and are prone to occur in AAV in storage.

01:20:09.680 --> 01:20:10.760
We can talk later.

01:20:10.760 --> 01:20:11.840
Of course, thank you.

01:20:13.120 --> 01:20:15.440
John, since we have a little bit of time,

01:20:16.440 --> 01:20:17.280
I have a question.

01:20:17.280 --> 01:20:21.200
So, do you have any specific amino acid residues

01:20:21.200 --> 01:20:26.200
that are involved in on this contact point for the PAPS?

01:20:28.400 --> 01:20:31.440
We have identified those, and for the sake

01:20:31.440 --> 01:20:33.200
of my dissertation, I'm holding onto them

01:20:33.200 --> 01:20:36.000
until I get it all worked out,

01:20:36.000 --> 01:20:39.560
but I will be publishing that, and that will be available.

01:20:39.560 --> 01:20:40.600
All right.

01:20:40.600 --> 01:20:45.560
Is there any more questions for John, if not?

01:20:45.560 --> 01:20:46.560
Thank you, John.

01:20:46.560 --> 01:20:47.400
Thank you.

01:20:47.400 --> 01:20:48.240
Thank you.

01:20:48.240 --> 01:20:49.080
Thank you.

01:20:52.920 --> 01:20:56.360
So, with that, I would like to invite our next speaker,

01:20:56.360 --> 01:21:00.080
Simon Pecorre, and he will be talking

01:21:00.080 --> 01:21:03.440
about a modular capsid engineering platform

01:21:03.440 --> 01:21:07.640
to confirm neutralizing antibody evasion to CNS

01:21:07.640 --> 01:21:10.520
and muscle-targeted AAVs.

01:21:10.520 --> 01:21:11.360
Welcome.

01:21:11.360 --> 01:21:12.200
Thank you.

01:21:12.200 --> 01:21:15.040
Thanks for the opportunity to present our work today.

01:21:15.040 --> 01:21:20.040
So, there are several ways we can deliver genes to the CNS.

01:21:20.040 --> 01:21:21.800
You can go intraparenchymal.

01:21:21.800 --> 01:21:25.080
You can deliver into the CSF, or go intravenous.

01:21:25.080 --> 01:21:29.560
And because some brain diseases impact the entire brain,

01:21:29.560 --> 01:21:32.560
we believe that there is a need to deliver cargos

01:21:32.560 --> 01:21:35.480
and as many neurons of the CNS as possible.

01:21:35.480 --> 01:21:38.520
And we believe that the only practical way of achieving that

01:21:38.520 --> 01:21:44.000
is to use the vasculature, so the third one, and the GOIV.

01:21:44.000 --> 01:21:45.480
But there are a couple of challenges

01:21:45.480 --> 01:21:50.960
in achieving CNS, it's systemic gene delivery to the CNS.

01:21:50.960 --> 01:21:53.320
One is that naturally occurring serotypes

01:21:53.320 --> 01:21:56.000
do not cross the blood-brain barrier efficiently.

01:21:56.000 --> 01:21:57.400
And the second one is that they're

01:21:57.400 --> 01:22:00.280
susceptible to a neutralizing antibodies resulting

01:22:00.280 --> 01:22:02.760
from pre-exposure to the virus.

01:22:02.760 --> 01:22:04.720
So in the next few minutes, I'm going to tell you

01:22:04.720 --> 01:22:08.800
how our lab tackled both challenges sequentially.

01:22:08.800 --> 01:22:13.360
And we'll start with the BBB crossing.

01:22:13.360 --> 01:22:18.040
So to cross the BBB, my colleagues Ken Shan, Helen Wong,

01:22:18.040 --> 01:22:21.480
and Jean Lien reprogrammed AV-9 to bind to the human

01:22:21.480 --> 01:22:23.240
transfer and receptor.

01:22:23.240 --> 01:22:26.840
And this led to the discovery of a second generation human

01:22:26.840 --> 01:22:28.560
transfer and receptor binding capsid

01:22:28.560 --> 01:22:33.200
that we call BHGF01B2, aka TF01CAPX.

01:22:33.200 --> 01:22:35.480
So if you want more information about this capsid,

01:22:35.480 --> 01:22:38.000
I strongly recommend that you go to talk to my colleague Ken

01:22:38.000 --> 01:22:38.500
Shan.

01:22:38.500 --> 01:22:39.160
He's right here.

01:22:39.160 --> 01:22:42.320
He'll be presenting this afternoon his poster number

01:22:42.320 --> 01:22:47.760
3037 on BHGF01B2 characterization.

01:22:47.760 --> 01:22:50.640
I'm just going to share a little piece of data here.

01:22:50.640 --> 01:22:54.360
This second generation capsid is capable of efficient

01:22:54.360 --> 01:22:57.520
transduction of the brain in human transfer and receptor

01:22:57.520 --> 01:23:02.240
knocking animals, as seen on the right of that slide here.

01:23:02.240 --> 01:23:04.200
It outperforms the first generation

01:23:04.200 --> 01:23:07.520
variant BHGF01 as well as AV-9, and it mainly

01:23:07.520 --> 01:23:09.920
transduces neurons.

01:23:09.920 --> 01:23:11.560
And what's interesting to notice here

01:23:11.560 --> 01:23:16.840
is we work in that type of study as a dose of 2E10VG

01:23:16.840 --> 01:23:23.880
per kilogram, 2E12, sorry, which is about 50-fold lower

01:23:23.880 --> 01:23:25.480
than Zolgenzma.

01:23:25.480 --> 01:23:27.920
So lowering the dose is great because it

01:23:27.920 --> 01:23:30.440
reduces the risk of toxicity.

01:23:30.440 --> 01:23:32.640
But at the same time, if you lower the dose,

01:23:32.640 --> 01:23:35.040
then you have more neutralizing antibodies per capsid.

01:23:35.040 --> 01:23:39.520
And the neutralizing antibody problem becomes even bigger.

01:23:39.520 --> 01:23:42.880
So that brings us to a second challenge.

01:23:42.880 --> 01:23:48.320
So how did we take BHGF01B2 and further engineer it

01:23:48.320 --> 01:23:51.720
to evade neutralizing antibodies?

01:23:51.720 --> 01:23:56.880
So I want to start with a model of BHGF01B2 capsid.

01:23:56.880 --> 01:23:58.520
It's an alpha-4-3 model.

01:23:58.520 --> 01:24:00.640
And in blue is represented the region that

01:24:00.640 --> 01:24:05.320
was engineered to bind to the human transfer and receptor.

01:24:05.320 --> 01:24:07.880
So as you can see, it's a very small region of the capsid.

01:24:07.880 --> 01:24:11.000
And the rest of the capsid actually looks like AV-9.

01:24:11.000 --> 01:24:14.840
So to make it evade neutralizing antibodies,

01:24:14.840 --> 01:24:17.960
we kind of decided that we had to disrupt

01:24:17.960 --> 01:24:22.520
as many anti-AV-9 antibody epitopes as possible,

01:24:22.520 --> 01:24:27.120
possibly with as few modifications as we could.

01:24:27.120 --> 01:24:30.120
And we looked in nature and found

01:24:30.120 --> 01:24:32.680
what strategy that was pretty appealing that

01:24:32.680 --> 01:24:35.840
consists in modulating surface charge.

01:24:35.840 --> 01:24:38.200
So we have seen that during COVID with the Omicron

01:24:38.200 --> 01:24:41.200
variant that actually was shown to evade

01:24:41.200 --> 01:24:44.600
the great majority of antibody-based drugs that

01:24:44.600 --> 01:24:47.520
were developed to neutralize the original strain.

01:24:47.520 --> 01:24:50.280
And this was attributed to a few mutations in the protein

01:24:50.280 --> 01:24:53.880
spike that created a positively charged patch.

01:24:53.880 --> 01:24:59.720
So the question became, can we apply this strategy to BHGF01B2

01:24:59.720 --> 01:25:02.440
in order to make it evade neutralizing antibodies

01:25:02.440 --> 01:25:06.120
while retaining its BBB crossing phenotype?

01:25:06.120 --> 01:25:10.600
To do that, we started from the parental capsid, V2.

01:25:10.600 --> 01:25:13.280
We identified surface-exposed target residues

01:25:13.280 --> 01:25:15.800
that we mutated to either positively charge

01:25:15.800 --> 01:25:18.880
neutral or negatively charge residue.

01:25:18.880 --> 01:25:20.760
We took the resulting library, and we

01:25:20.760 --> 01:25:22.640
screened it for production fitness,

01:25:22.640 --> 01:25:25.820
for in vitro antibody evasion, as well as blood-brain

01:25:25.820 --> 01:25:27.960
barrier crossing in human transfer and receptor

01:25:27.960 --> 01:25:29.640
knocking animals.

01:25:29.640 --> 01:25:32.240
We did that in two rounds, modified a total of seven

01:25:32.240 --> 01:25:34.960
capsid regions, and characterized the selected

01:25:34.960 --> 01:25:37.000
variants individually.

01:25:37.000 --> 01:25:40.000
And to increase the probability of identifying

01:25:40.000 --> 01:25:42.800
capsid scaffolds that would be compatible with other loop

01:25:42.800 --> 01:25:45.760
eight modifications, we also added

01:25:46.400 --> 01:25:49.200
three different peptides crossing the BBB in mice

01:25:49.200 --> 01:25:53.000
using three different receptors, so EBBI28 and 9P31,

01:25:53.000 --> 01:25:55.360
to the original library.

01:25:55.360 --> 01:25:58.480
So first, we can walk through the NGS data.

01:25:58.480 --> 01:26:01.880
Here's the production fitness profile of the library.

01:26:01.880 --> 01:26:06.480
As you can see, AV9 and V2 have like pretty similar scores.

01:26:06.480 --> 01:26:09.000
And we selected variants within that blue regions

01:26:09.000 --> 01:26:13.480
that produced within a reasonable distance of AV9.

01:26:13.520 --> 01:26:16.840
We looked at brain transduction in TF4C knocking animals,

01:26:16.840 --> 01:26:19.920
and here each dot represents a variant with four

01:26:19.920 --> 01:26:23.080
codon replicates, and you're looking at the

01:26:23.080 --> 01:26:26.360
transduction score in one mice versus the other.

01:26:26.360 --> 01:26:30.080
As expected, BHTF1V2 are performed AV9,

01:26:30.080 --> 01:26:33.360
and we selected variants within that green region.

01:26:33.360 --> 01:26:36.800
Last, we look at antibody evasion in H2993 cells.

01:26:37.960 --> 01:26:42.000
And here I'm showing you the transduction score

01:26:42.000 --> 01:26:44.720
in the absence of IVIG on the x-axis

01:26:44.720 --> 01:26:48.120
versus presence of IVIG on the y-axis.

01:26:48.120 --> 01:26:51.760
And we selected variants that had like a high score

01:26:51.760 --> 01:26:54.440
in the presence of neutralizing antibodies.

01:26:54.440 --> 01:26:56.640
So now when you take the intersection of these three

01:26:56.640 --> 01:26:59.400
regions, you find a limited set of variants,

01:26:59.400 --> 01:27:04.240
including one that we called BHTF1V2E for evasion,

01:27:04.240 --> 01:27:07.040
and that we characterized more in detail.

01:27:07.920 --> 01:27:10.320
So the first thing we did was to look at the surface

01:27:10.320 --> 01:27:13.520
charge profile of V2E, and what we could observe

01:27:13.520 --> 01:27:16.600
is that it was pretty different from the parental capsid

01:27:16.600 --> 01:27:20.320
as expected, considering the strategy that was used

01:27:20.320 --> 01:27:22.000
in that project.

01:27:22.000 --> 01:27:25.000
So now, what is the impact of those modifications

01:27:25.000 --> 01:27:26.600
on tropism?

01:27:26.600 --> 01:27:28.240
We looked at that in human transfer

01:27:28.240 --> 01:27:30.640
and receptor knocking animals.

01:27:30.640 --> 01:27:33.640
You're looking at a comparative analysis of V2E,

01:27:33.640 --> 01:27:36.360
V2 and AV9 in human transfer and knocking mice,

01:27:36.360 --> 01:27:39.120
and you're looking at the transduction data on the left,

01:27:39.120 --> 01:27:41.680
and VG by distribution data on the right.

01:27:41.680 --> 01:27:44.920
And as you can see is that the BBB crossing phenotype

01:27:44.920 --> 01:27:49.160
of V2E is maintained in V2E in spite of the differences

01:27:49.160 --> 01:27:50.240
in surface charge.

01:27:51.240 --> 01:27:54.400
Let's look at antibody evasion now in vitro.

01:27:54.400 --> 01:27:59.400
So we did that with 73 human sera from individual donors,

01:28:00.080 --> 01:28:05.080
and we used AV9, AV5, and BHTF1V2S controls.

01:28:05.080 --> 01:28:07.560
So here I'm plotting all the neutralization curves

01:28:07.560 --> 01:28:10.800
that were measured for sera neutralizing AV9,

01:28:10.800 --> 01:28:15.160
color coded by their IC50, red meaning high neutralization

01:28:15.160 --> 01:28:17.600
and blue meaning low neutralization.

01:28:17.600 --> 01:28:20.200
So as you can see, the profile are pretty similar

01:28:20.200 --> 01:28:23.240
for V9 and V2, but you see a lot of curves

01:28:23.240 --> 01:28:25.280
that get like right shifted for V2E,

01:28:25.280 --> 01:28:27.800
suggesting some level of antibody evasion.

01:28:28.640 --> 01:28:31.240
And when you take that and measure the IC50

01:28:31.240 --> 01:28:35.240
for all these human sera, they are all represented

01:28:36.040 --> 01:28:37.800
as columns in that heat map.

01:28:38.760 --> 01:28:41.800
You see that the IC50 values go down across the board

01:28:41.800 --> 01:28:45.520
for V2E compared to the other three serotypes

01:28:45.520 --> 01:28:49.680
with actually 40% of the human sera

01:28:49.680 --> 01:28:54.680
that neutralize BHTF1V2 that exhibit IC50 below

01:28:55.160 --> 01:28:59.440
or limit of detection of one of four for BHTF1V2E.

01:28:59.440 --> 01:29:00.360
So that's good news.

01:29:00.360 --> 01:29:02.000
It means like we're potentially able

01:29:02.000 --> 01:29:05.400
to expand the patient pool for CNS gene therapy,

01:29:05.400 --> 01:29:09.520
but we wanted to validate these results first in vivo.

01:29:09.520 --> 01:29:14.520
So we worked on a model where we passively immunized

01:29:14.800 --> 01:29:16.800
human transfer and receptor knocking animals

01:29:16.800 --> 01:29:19.520
with IgG pre-fight from human sera.

01:29:19.520 --> 01:29:24.520
A day later, we co-injected AV9, V2E and V2E IV.

01:29:24.520 --> 01:29:27.680
And at day 21, we looked at function in the brain

01:29:27.680 --> 01:29:29.120
by RTD PCR.

01:29:30.120 --> 01:29:32.720
So let's look at the human sera first

01:29:32.720 --> 01:29:36.400
from which we pre-fight IgG for the passive immunization.

01:29:36.400 --> 01:29:38.240
You can see first that they have different

01:29:38.240 --> 01:29:41.560
neutralization potencies with C being monetizing

01:29:41.560 --> 01:29:43.640
then B and A overall.

01:29:43.640 --> 01:29:48.640
And then you can also see that BHTF1V2E evades this sera

01:29:49.240 --> 01:29:51.320
to a greater extent than the other two

01:29:51.320 --> 01:29:52.800
because it's IC50 value.

01:29:52.800 --> 01:29:56.040
It's actually lower when measured in vitro.

01:29:56.040 --> 01:30:00.720
So now let's look at the sera of mice

01:30:00.720 --> 01:30:03.480
that were passively immunized with these IgG.

01:30:03.480 --> 01:30:06.600
So again, you can see that the mice that receive

01:30:06.600 --> 01:30:10.200
the compound C or have a serum that's monetizing

01:30:10.200 --> 01:30:13.000
to all three capsids compared to B and A.

01:30:13.000 --> 01:30:16.640
And you also see those response at the 30 milligram dose

01:30:16.640 --> 01:30:19.400
versus 10 milligram IgG per animal.

01:30:19.400 --> 01:30:24.080
And again, BHTF1V2E show IC50 below

01:30:24.120 --> 01:30:28.440
or limit of detection of one to 20 in that particular assay.

01:30:28.440 --> 01:30:30.360
So now let's look at function.

01:30:30.360 --> 01:30:33.960
This is the function in the absence of IgG.

01:30:33.960 --> 01:30:38.960
So as you can see, V2 and V2E as expected outperform AV9.

01:30:38.980 --> 01:30:40.800
And now when you look at all the conditions

01:30:40.800 --> 01:30:43.280
and you normalize that score to the saline control,

01:30:43.280 --> 01:30:46.480
you can see that you have a greater rescue

01:30:46.480 --> 01:30:51.480
of your brain signal in animals treated with BHTF1V2E

01:30:52.040 --> 01:30:55.880
as opposed to V2 and AV9 with a full change ranging

01:30:55.880 --> 01:30:59.480
from three to 40 fold in serum C and B.

01:30:59.480 --> 01:31:00.960
A wasn't neutralizing enough

01:31:00.960 --> 01:31:03.640
that we could really observe anything in vivo.

01:31:03.640 --> 01:31:06.920
So that enabled us to conclude that this serotype

01:31:06.920 --> 01:31:10.300
was also capable of antibody evasion in vivo.

01:31:12.040 --> 01:31:13.680
We looked at production titers

01:31:13.680 --> 01:31:15.560
in suspension and adherence cells.

01:31:15.560 --> 01:31:17.760
And in the absence of optimization,

01:31:17.760 --> 01:31:22.760
we were able to produce V2E within a two-fold of AV9.

01:31:22.760 --> 01:31:24.160
And we were able to also show

01:31:24.160 --> 01:31:27.520
that it was compatible with porous AVX, AV pure AV9

01:31:27.520 --> 01:31:31.520
for a capture affinity chromatography, but not porous AV9.

01:31:33.760 --> 01:31:36.760
Last, we wanted to see whether

01:31:36.760 --> 01:31:39.040
some of our antibody evading scaffolds

01:31:39.040 --> 01:31:42.520
were compatible with muscle targeting peptides.

01:31:42.520 --> 01:31:46.640
So we identified five potential candidates

01:31:46.640 --> 01:31:51.160
that we combined with two muscle peptide myoav4A

01:31:51.160 --> 01:31:54.520
as well as one that we call RGD1.

01:31:54.520 --> 01:31:58.000
And we characterized the resulting variants.

01:31:58.000 --> 01:32:01.480
And so we were able to show that one was compatible

01:32:01.480 --> 01:32:03.680
with both and we followed up on this one

01:32:03.680 --> 01:32:06.200
with the RGD1 peptide only.

01:32:06.200 --> 01:32:09.360
And if you look at antibody neutralization in vitro,

01:32:10.840 --> 01:32:13.960
the second generation that we call RGD1 Gen2

01:32:13.960 --> 01:32:16.080
was able to evade preexisting antibodies

01:32:16.080 --> 01:32:20.200
in about 32% of the human Sierra that neutralized RGD1.

01:32:20.200 --> 01:32:22.040
And this year, we further evolved it

01:32:22.040 --> 01:32:24.960
and we went up to around 56%.

01:32:26.720 --> 01:32:30.440
And last, we looked at function in C57 animals

01:32:30.440 --> 01:32:35.440
and we saw no major difference in muscle between RGD1,

01:32:35.600 --> 01:32:38.400
RGD1 Gen2 and Gen3.

01:32:40.280 --> 01:32:45.280
So to finish, this is actually a gift

01:32:45.280 --> 01:32:48.360
of the surface charge modulation between RGD,

01:32:48.360 --> 01:32:50.960
RGD1 Gen2 and Gen3.

01:32:50.960 --> 01:32:52.640
And this brings us to a conclusion

01:32:52.640 --> 01:32:55.240
and we applied a charge alteration with a genesis

01:32:55.240 --> 01:32:58.080
to CNS and muscle tropic capsids.

01:32:58.080 --> 01:33:01.680
The resulting variants maintained their enhanced tropism.

01:33:01.680 --> 01:33:05.280
They were able to produce within two-fold of AV9

01:33:05.280 --> 01:33:09.160
and they evaded 40 to 56% of the human Sierra

01:33:09.160 --> 01:33:10.600
that neutralized AV9.

01:33:10.600 --> 01:33:12.760
So this method is potentially applicable

01:33:12.760 --> 01:33:16.480
to any tissue-specific capsids.

01:33:16.480 --> 01:33:20.600
And with this, I would like to thank all my lab mates

01:33:20.600 --> 01:33:24.440
and colleagues and collaborators from the Deverman Lab

01:33:24.440 --> 01:33:27.000
and the Varadmini Care Lab at the Broad,

01:33:27.000 --> 01:33:29.940
especially Ken Shan, Jean Lien, and Jenselyn Johnston,

01:33:29.940 --> 01:33:31.600
who took care of all the NBSA,

01:33:31.600 --> 01:33:34.520
as well as Pam Brower and John Harvey

01:33:34.520 --> 01:33:35.600
for the manufacturing.

01:33:35.600 --> 01:33:37.760
And I'll be happy to take any questions.

01:33:38.560 --> 01:33:39.400
Thank you.

01:33:44.400 --> 01:33:45.520
Thank you, Simon.

01:33:45.520 --> 01:33:48.040
And we have questions over here.

01:33:48.040 --> 01:33:49.640
Excellent talk.

01:33:49.640 --> 01:33:53.440
So I think you said you looked in seven different areas.

01:33:53.440 --> 01:33:57.040
How many edits did the best capsid end up having?

01:33:58.200 --> 01:33:59.280
How many mutations?

01:33:59.280 --> 01:34:00.120
Yes.

01:34:02.320 --> 01:34:04.440
More than you can count on, let's say,

01:34:04.440 --> 01:34:07.240
between 10 and 50.

01:34:07.240 --> 01:34:08.400
Okay, great, thank you.

01:34:11.800 --> 01:34:12.880
Thanks for your talk.

01:34:13.840 --> 01:34:16.960
I have a conceptual question on the results

01:34:16.960 --> 01:34:20.840
of your library that looks like you had the same approach

01:34:20.840 --> 01:34:25.080
of modifying the charge surface for all your library on you,

01:34:25.080 --> 01:34:27.680
showcase some kind of positive candidate.

01:34:27.680 --> 01:34:29.440
Did you try to extract the negative one

01:34:29.440 --> 01:34:32.120
on to redox the neutralizing antibodies

01:34:32.120 --> 01:34:35.760
to try to identify which position was actually involving

01:34:35.880 --> 01:34:39.240
that kind of conceptual evading of that?

01:34:39.240 --> 01:34:43.120
When do you plan to finally make this data accessible?

01:34:45.280 --> 01:34:47.840
You're talking about the dual production fitness

01:34:47.840 --> 01:34:48.800
distribution?

01:34:48.800 --> 01:34:52.760
No, it's in the way you modify your variant,

01:34:52.760 --> 01:34:55.920
you consider on your top right of your graphics,

01:34:55.920 --> 01:34:58.960
the selection of your top variant that is evading,

01:34:58.960 --> 01:35:02.120
but you may have several AVs vector

01:35:02.120 --> 01:35:04.720
that have a modification of the charge surface,

01:35:04.760 --> 01:35:07.040
but without evading the system.

01:35:07.040 --> 01:35:09.760
So you can envision to redox actually

01:35:09.760 --> 01:35:12.160
the neutralizing antibody itself to see

01:35:12.160 --> 01:35:14.120
what is the difference, functional difference

01:35:14.120 --> 01:35:17.000
between what works to what doesn't work finally

01:35:17.000 --> 01:35:22.000
at the level of your pure capsid to antibodies binding.

01:35:22.360 --> 01:35:26.960
Yeah, we initiated some work in that direction,

01:35:26.960 --> 01:35:30.160
but I think it's not as mature as you would expect.

01:35:30.160 --> 01:35:31.000
Thank you.

01:35:35.000 --> 01:35:37.080
So I don't see any more questions.

01:35:38.480 --> 01:35:40.320
Oh, there is one.

01:35:40.320 --> 01:35:41.760
Beautiful talk, Simo.

01:35:41.760 --> 01:35:42.600
Thanks.

01:35:42.600 --> 01:35:47.600
I love that you tested both AVX and the AV9 porous resin,

01:35:49.320 --> 01:35:51.160
and you saw a major difference there,

01:35:51.160 --> 01:35:54.440
but what about things like anion exchange columns

01:35:54.440 --> 01:35:55.880
and other downstream purificate?

01:35:55.880 --> 01:35:57.840
How do you think that this charge modifications

01:35:57.840 --> 01:35:59.880
will affect things like that?

01:35:59.880 --> 01:36:04.320
Yeah, TBG, we haven't looked into that either yet.

01:36:04.760 --> 01:36:05.880
Very great work.

01:36:05.880 --> 01:36:06.720
Thank you.

01:36:09.200 --> 01:36:10.040
Thank you.

01:36:14.040 --> 01:36:15.560
Thank you, Simo.

01:36:15.560 --> 01:36:20.480
So with that, we have our last presentation

01:36:20.480 --> 01:36:23.960
from Adrian Sevi, and he will be talking

01:36:23.960 --> 01:36:27.600
about one AAV capsid from Coyev,

01:36:27.600 --> 01:36:31.680
and he is from Coyev Therapeutics, and it's all yours.

01:36:31.680 --> 01:36:33.000
Thank you for the introduction,

01:36:33.000 --> 01:36:34.880
and first of all, thanks to the organizer

01:36:34.880 --> 01:36:37.560
for giving me the opportunity to present

01:36:37.560 --> 01:36:40.400
how we are redefining targeting gene therapy

01:36:40.400 --> 01:36:41.640
at Coyev Therapeutics.

01:36:43.720 --> 01:36:47.400
And today, we'll talk about capsid engineering.

01:36:47.400 --> 01:36:49.440
We are doing bioconjugation.

01:36:49.440 --> 01:36:50.280
Why?

01:36:50.280 --> 01:36:52.280
It's mainly to address the key challenges

01:36:52.280 --> 01:36:54.000
of IV injection.

01:36:54.000 --> 01:36:55.760
The first one is the targeting.

01:36:55.760 --> 01:36:58.760
How do we bring the vector to the tissue of interest?

01:36:59.760 --> 01:37:03.800
Bioconjugation allows more efficient conjugated capsid

01:37:03.800 --> 01:37:06.560
because we are looking for non-mechanisms.

01:37:06.560 --> 01:37:07.640
How do we do it?

01:37:07.640 --> 01:37:09.960
We first rationally design ligands

01:37:09.960 --> 01:37:13.560
that interact with human receptor with the non-mechanisms.

01:37:15.520 --> 01:37:17.280
And we have a very modular platform

01:37:17.280 --> 01:37:19.200
that is based on chemical conjugation,

01:37:19.200 --> 01:37:20.960
creating a covalent bond.

01:37:20.960 --> 01:37:24.520
It's irreversible and does not require any catalyst.

01:37:24.520 --> 01:37:26.280
This is very modular.

01:37:26.280 --> 01:37:28.600
We can really fine-tune the coupling level

01:37:28.600 --> 01:37:30.640
to perfectly match the requirements

01:37:30.640 --> 01:37:32.560
of the targeted receptors.

01:37:32.560 --> 01:37:35.600
And as we are completely decoupling the tropisms

01:37:35.600 --> 01:37:39.240
from the capsid, we can integrate this platform

01:37:39.240 --> 01:37:44.240
to any existing AVs, wild type, engineers, and so on.

01:37:46.840 --> 01:37:49.600
At Coyev, we have many different kinds of ligands

01:37:49.600 --> 01:37:52.880
from small molecules to peptides to VHH.

01:37:52.880 --> 01:37:54.840
Each of them is rationally designed

01:37:55.840 --> 01:38:00.000
to target a specific receptor and also to allow

01:38:00.000 --> 01:38:02.040
a cross-reactivity among species

01:38:02.040 --> 01:38:04.800
and mostly between human and NHPs.

01:38:04.800 --> 01:38:08.600
To simplify it, you can see it like antibody drug conjugate

01:38:08.600 --> 01:38:10.040
but applied to AVs.

01:38:11.280 --> 01:38:13.320
So once we perform our bioconjugation,

01:38:13.320 --> 01:38:16.440
we are creating a new generation of targeted vectors.

01:38:16.440 --> 01:38:18.480
And at Coyev, we have three main focuses

01:38:18.480 --> 01:38:21.680
in ophthalmology, in CNS, and for muscle diseases.

01:38:22.680 --> 01:38:25.160
I don't know if you had the chance to attempt earlier

01:38:25.160 --> 01:38:28.280
this morning to our new supra-coryidal capsids

01:38:28.280 --> 01:38:30.680
but if you want, do not hesitate to be in touch.

01:38:30.680 --> 01:38:34.880
And I also strongly recommend you to attempt later today

01:38:34.880 --> 01:38:38.320
to the Poster 3026 from my colleague Lavagna

01:38:38.320 --> 01:38:40.720
that will present how the ligand conjugation

01:38:40.720 --> 01:38:43.320
is enabling cross-paces crossing

01:38:43.320 --> 01:38:45.200
through human TFR1 receptor.

01:38:47.040 --> 01:38:50.160
If we talk about the CNS and we have a look

01:38:50.160 --> 01:38:55.160
to our AV9 BBB crossings through TFR1 workflow,

01:38:55.280 --> 01:38:58.680
we first design the ligands and we can do that

01:38:58.680 --> 01:39:00.080
because we are doing bioconjugation.

01:39:00.080 --> 01:39:01.840
We are not interested by the capsid.

01:39:01.840 --> 01:39:04.000
It's really the ligands that we are working on it.

01:39:04.000 --> 01:39:06.440
We are really designed them to be specific

01:39:06.440 --> 01:39:09.360
for a human receptor but also to work in NHPs.

01:39:09.360 --> 01:39:12.000
And thanks to the bioconjugation and to our chemistry,

01:39:12.000 --> 01:39:15.280
we can really fine tune the coupling level.

01:39:15.280 --> 01:39:18.600
We can clearly increase how many ligands we put

01:39:18.600 --> 01:39:20.100
at the capsid surface and it's

01:39:20.100 --> 01:39:22.920
tightly managed control process.

01:39:22.920 --> 01:39:26.400
We then screened our conjugated AVs in vitro.

01:39:26.400 --> 01:39:28.120
In the example with two cell lines,

01:39:28.120 --> 01:39:30.920
the first one, a human expressing the transfer

01:39:30.920 --> 01:39:32.560
in one receptor cell line.

01:39:32.560 --> 01:39:35.480
And our two conjugated AV9 either with a ligand one

01:39:35.480 --> 01:39:38.080
or two have the same transaction efficiency

01:39:38.080 --> 01:39:41.240
as the published TFR1 benchmark.

01:39:41.240 --> 01:39:44.640
But if we switch to a Sino-expressing TFR1 cell line,

01:39:44.640 --> 01:39:48.480
our two conjugated AV9 either in blue or green,

01:39:48.520 --> 01:39:50.160
maintains their transaction efficiency

01:39:50.160 --> 01:39:53.400
whereas the published TFR1 benchmark does not.

01:39:54.800 --> 01:39:57.200
We then move to in vivo screening.

01:39:59.560 --> 01:40:02.760
And you know the AV9 is a poor brain transducer

01:40:02.760 --> 01:40:04.040
when injected by IV.

01:40:05.320 --> 01:40:08.720
But if you take the exact same AV9

01:40:08.720 --> 01:40:12.120
and you conjugate it with our TFR1 ligands,

01:40:12.120 --> 01:40:14.280
it's knocking humanized mice,

01:40:14.280 --> 01:40:16.120
you can see that the transaction efficiency

01:40:16.120 --> 01:40:19.600
is greatly improved with the world brain transduction.

01:40:21.080 --> 01:40:23.640
If we look at the RNA expression,

01:40:23.640 --> 01:40:27.720
we have a 40-fold difference in term of expression

01:40:27.720 --> 01:40:31.320
compared to AV9 in purple.

01:40:31.320 --> 01:40:34.960
And we are perfectly matching the published benchmark

01:40:34.960 --> 01:40:36.560
in term of brain transduction.

01:40:36.560 --> 01:40:37.920
But we also have a second effect.

01:40:37.920 --> 01:40:39.840
We have a very strong liver detargeting

01:40:39.840 --> 01:40:43.200
with our conjugated AV9 with more than 15 fold decrease

01:40:43.200 --> 01:40:44.200
in liver expression.

01:40:45.200 --> 01:40:47.760
Our platform is something that is very versatile,

01:40:47.760 --> 01:40:51.680
so we can move from the brain to the muscles.

01:40:51.680 --> 01:40:55.400
And if we take the exact same AV9 capsid backbone

01:40:55.400 --> 01:40:58.600
and with this time conjugated with a peptide

01:40:58.600 --> 01:41:00.120
that has been designed to interact

01:41:00.120 --> 01:41:02.000
with the integrin alpha V beta 6,

01:41:02.000 --> 01:41:05.720
we obtain a conjugated AV9 targeting these integrins.

01:41:06.600 --> 01:41:10.080
And this time we have a 300 fold increase

01:41:10.080 --> 01:41:11.480
in the gastrocnemius muscles

01:41:11.600 --> 01:41:16.440
and the 200 fold increase in the quadriceps,

01:41:16.440 --> 01:41:20.640
both reaching the published beta 6 benchmark again.

01:41:23.400 --> 01:41:25.680
So the Alligator platform is something

01:41:25.680 --> 01:41:28.040
that is very versatile, it's very tunable,

01:41:28.040 --> 01:41:29.840
and it's highly scalable.

01:41:29.840 --> 01:41:30.920
We can do that because again,

01:41:30.920 --> 01:41:32.160
we are doing bio-conjugations.

01:41:32.160 --> 01:41:33.880
We are not that interested by the capsid,

01:41:33.880 --> 01:41:35.640
only by the ligands.

01:41:35.640 --> 01:41:39.360
And we wanted to go beyond in term of addressing

01:41:39.360 --> 01:41:40.480
the IV challenges.

01:41:40.480 --> 01:41:41.880
So the first one was the targeting,

01:41:41.880 --> 01:41:44.200
I think that we can fix it easily now,

01:41:44.200 --> 01:41:45.640
but we wanted to go really beyond

01:41:45.640 --> 01:41:47.720
and address two other challenges.

01:41:47.720 --> 01:41:49.880
The first one was the immunovasion.

01:41:49.880 --> 01:41:53.720
And to do that, we created one AV capsid family,

01:41:53.720 --> 01:41:55.240
it's kind of proof of concept.

01:41:56.480 --> 01:41:59.600
It has been designed to completely

01:41:59.600 --> 01:42:02.840
decorelate the tropisms from the capsid.

01:42:02.840 --> 01:42:06.040
We wanted a kind of complete neutral shell,

01:42:06.040 --> 01:42:07.160
and it has been engineered

01:42:07.160 --> 01:42:10.360
from low seroprevalence serotypes in humans

01:42:10.360 --> 01:42:13.360
to address the immune response problems.

01:42:13.360 --> 01:42:14.480
And we also engineered them

01:42:14.480 --> 01:42:17.600
to completely obtain a lever day targeting.

01:42:17.600 --> 01:42:20.120
So we had a neutral shell

01:42:20.120 --> 01:42:22.200
without any interaction on its own.

01:42:22.200 --> 01:42:23.800
And thanks to the Alligator platform,

01:42:23.800 --> 01:42:27.400
we can strongly retarget them to tissue of interest.

01:42:29.240 --> 01:42:33.040
We first validated our one AV capsid by IV injections.

01:42:35.000 --> 01:42:37.880
Quite a high dose for 10 to the 13 VG per KIG

01:42:37.920 --> 01:42:40.920
with the luciferous transgene under the KAK promoter.

01:42:40.920 --> 01:42:42.920
And we compare it to the AV9

01:42:42.920 --> 01:42:46.040
in both male and female wild type mice.

01:42:46.040 --> 01:42:49.320
As expected for the AV9 groups,

01:42:49.320 --> 01:42:51.920
we saw very strong expression in the lever,

01:42:51.920 --> 01:42:54.520
whereas the expression was barely detectable

01:42:54.520 --> 01:42:55.600
with our one AV.

01:42:56.640 --> 01:42:59.320
We wanted to know where went the vectors.

01:42:59.320 --> 01:43:01.680
So three weeks but in election at Sacrifice,

01:43:01.680 --> 01:43:04.840
we look at the vector copy number in different tissue.

01:43:04.840 --> 01:43:08.600
And in the lever, we have 800 for lower copy number

01:43:08.600 --> 01:43:10.200
compared to the AV9

01:43:10.200 --> 01:43:14.080
with an astonishing 0.03 vector copy per cell.

01:43:14.080 --> 01:43:16.320
This I believe is a truly worthy targeting.

01:43:17.240 --> 01:43:21.240
Our non-conjugated one AV does not cross the BBB on its own.

01:43:21.240 --> 01:43:23.360
We have a thousand for lower expression,

01:43:23.360 --> 01:43:25.760
not expression, vector copy number in the brain.

01:43:25.760 --> 01:43:29.720
And it was quite the same in every peripheral organs

01:43:29.720 --> 01:43:31.560
that we have looked at.

01:43:31.560 --> 01:43:34.000
So we know we have this neutral shell.

01:43:34.000 --> 01:43:35.360
What can we do with it?

01:43:35.360 --> 01:43:37.280
And what is happening if we conjugated it

01:43:37.280 --> 01:43:39.680
with the TFR1 ligands?

01:43:39.680 --> 01:43:44.360
We first confirm an unprecedented lever de-targeting

01:43:44.360 --> 01:43:46.400
with a thousand for lower expression.

01:43:46.400 --> 01:43:49.520
In the lever, you can see the AV9 expression,

01:43:49.520 --> 01:43:50.640
the lever in purple,

01:43:50.640 --> 01:43:53.160
and our conjugated TFR1 when AV capsid

01:43:53.160 --> 01:43:55.280
is here with a thousand fold.

01:43:55.280 --> 01:43:57.680
In the heart, we have 400 fold.

01:43:58.920 --> 01:44:01.680
But this conjugated one AV is perfectly able

01:44:01.680 --> 01:44:05.120
to cross the BBB and to express in the brain.

01:44:05.120 --> 01:44:06.840
When we compare to the AV9 on the left

01:44:06.840 --> 01:44:09.520
and the TFR1 conjugated one AV on the right,

01:44:09.520 --> 01:44:11.760
I think it's quite clear that this vector is able

01:44:11.760 --> 01:44:15.400
to cross the BBB and to express in the deep brain regions.

01:44:15.400 --> 01:44:18.680
And if we zoom in, we know that the AV9

01:44:18.680 --> 01:44:20.680
does not have any neuronal tropisms,

01:44:20.680 --> 01:44:23.120
whereas our conjugated one AV

01:44:23.120 --> 01:44:25.480
has a very strong neuronal tropism.

01:44:26.240 --> 01:44:31.240
We continue with beta-6 integrins targeting peptides.

01:44:31.240 --> 01:44:33.000
In wild type mice this time,

01:44:33.000 --> 01:44:36.040
we confirm again a very strong lever de-targeting

01:44:36.040 --> 01:44:38.920
with a 500 fold compared to AV9.

01:44:38.920 --> 01:44:41.240
The non-conjugated one AV is in red

01:44:41.240 --> 01:44:42.880
and the conjugated is in blue.

01:44:43.880 --> 01:44:46.120
In both muscles, quadriceps and gastrocnemius,

01:44:46.120 --> 01:44:48.120
we have not been able to detect any expression

01:44:48.120 --> 01:44:50.000
of the non-conjugated vectors.

01:44:50.000 --> 01:44:52.320
But once it's conjugated with the beta-6

01:44:52.320 --> 01:44:53.840
targeting TFR1,

01:44:54.080 --> 01:44:59.120
this time we are between five and 10 fold higher

01:44:59.120 --> 01:45:01.600
in term of expression compared to AV9.

01:45:04.440 --> 01:45:05.880
Because we are doing bio-conjugation

01:45:05.880 --> 01:45:07.880
and we are working on the ligands,

01:45:07.880 --> 01:45:10.480
we have been able to create this first generation

01:45:10.480 --> 01:45:11.680
of one AV capsid.

01:45:11.680 --> 01:45:15.880
It's the very first capsid with no tropisms on its own.

01:45:15.880 --> 01:45:17.720
It's quite inert.

01:45:17.720 --> 01:45:20.160
It has an unprecedented lever de-targeting.

01:45:20.160 --> 01:45:21.880
It's highly manufacturable.

01:45:21.880 --> 01:45:24.240
It's very similar to AV9 in term of yield.

01:45:25.280 --> 01:45:27.560
It's based on low seroprevalent serotypes,

01:45:27.560 --> 01:45:30.880
but thanks to the alligator platform and the chemistry,

01:45:30.880 --> 01:45:33.080
we can really target the vector on demand

01:45:33.080 --> 01:45:35.800
only in the tissue of interest.

01:45:38.960 --> 01:45:42.360
In conclusion, the alligator platform allows us

01:45:42.360 --> 01:45:45.360
to have cross reactive ligands among species

01:45:45.360 --> 01:45:49.560
with a very strong lever and very strong tissue targeting.

01:45:49.560 --> 01:45:52.840
We went from AV9 to benchmark expression level

01:45:52.840 --> 01:45:55.200
in both the CNS and in mussels.

01:45:56.720 --> 01:45:58.680
And we believe that the combination

01:45:58.680 --> 01:46:01.520
of the bio-conjugation, the alligator platform,

01:46:01.520 --> 01:46:04.320
and the one AV, we will pave the way

01:46:04.320 --> 01:46:06.040
of a certain generation of vectors

01:46:06.040 --> 01:46:08.240
that is safer and more potent.

01:46:08.240 --> 01:46:09.240
Thank you very much.

01:46:09.240 --> 01:46:11.640
And I would like to thank all co-members

01:46:11.640 --> 01:46:15.120
for this amazing work and mainly the bio-conjugation team

01:46:15.120 --> 01:46:18.000
which is here because without the girls,

01:46:18.000 --> 01:46:19.680
nothing would have been possible.

01:46:19.680 --> 01:46:20.520
Thank you.

01:46:20.520 --> 01:46:21.360
Thank you.

01:46:27.360 --> 01:46:28.280
Very interesting work.

01:46:28.280 --> 01:46:29.120
Thank you.

01:46:30.080 --> 01:46:33.600
I am a bit concerned about the generalization

01:46:33.600 --> 01:46:36.320
claim of this platform,

01:46:36.320 --> 01:46:38.880
given that the transference receptor as far as I know

01:46:38.880 --> 01:46:43.240
is judging by symbol linear peptide.

01:46:43.240 --> 01:46:46.800
So how do we feel the generalization

01:46:46.800 --> 01:46:50.760
to more complex receptor requirements

01:46:50.760 --> 01:46:54.000
like things that requires conformational rigidity

01:46:54.000 --> 01:46:56.480
or things that require limberization,

01:46:56.480 --> 01:46:58.880
depending on judgment or things that require

01:46:58.880 --> 01:47:00.560
ligand clustering?

01:47:01.760 --> 01:47:05.000
How you feel about this?

01:47:05.000 --> 01:47:06.760
I'm not completely sure, to be honest,

01:47:06.760 --> 01:47:09.280
to have fully understood your question.

01:47:09.280 --> 01:47:13.760
Very simply, how do you think this would generalize

01:47:13.760 --> 01:47:18.760
to other receptors that have more complex requirements

01:47:19.160 --> 01:47:20.680
to get in judging?

01:47:20.680 --> 01:47:23.800
That can be either more complex ligand,

01:47:23.800 --> 01:47:26.920
some has conformational requirements

01:47:26.920 --> 01:47:29.640
or clustering of more ligands,

01:47:29.640 --> 01:47:31.320
or...

01:47:31.320 --> 01:47:32.160
I get it.

01:47:32.160 --> 01:47:33.440
...limberization, things.

01:47:33.440 --> 01:47:37.000
Yeah, again, we are decorating the capsid.

01:47:37.000 --> 01:47:38.960
We are first screening the ligands

01:47:38.960 --> 01:47:40.480
to match the receptors.

01:47:40.480 --> 01:47:43.040
We know that our ligands are reactive

01:47:43.040 --> 01:47:44.680
with a receptor of interest,

01:47:44.680 --> 01:47:46.800
and then we conjugate them to the capsid.

01:47:51.800 --> 01:47:54.200
Hi, Dmitri from Ring Therapeutics.

01:47:54.200 --> 01:47:57.000
Very nice talk and very cool platform.

01:47:57.000 --> 01:48:00.480
I'm curious, with the TFR data you showed,

01:48:00.480 --> 01:48:02.240
since you mentioned that there's not

01:48:02.240 --> 01:48:05.000
kind of an intrinsic tropism of the capsid,

01:48:05.000 --> 01:48:10.000
how much more is the TFR doing after the BBB crossing

01:48:10.000 --> 01:48:12.360
to give you the actual transduction of the neurons?

01:48:12.480 --> 01:48:13.840
How are you actually targeting neurons?

01:48:13.840 --> 01:48:15.680
Is there additional liganding,

01:48:15.680 --> 01:48:17.880
or is it just an increased concentration in the brain

01:48:17.880 --> 01:48:19.280
that is leading to that transduction?

01:48:19.280 --> 01:48:20.440
It's a very good question.

01:48:20.440 --> 01:48:21.280
I can't answer it.

01:48:21.280 --> 01:48:22.720
It's a very preliminary work,

01:48:22.720 --> 01:48:24.560
but we are looking into it.

01:48:24.560 --> 01:48:25.400
Thank you.

01:48:28.120 --> 01:48:29.160
Hello, great talk.

01:48:29.160 --> 01:48:31.320
Hugh from there, Therapeutics.

01:48:31.320 --> 01:48:32.560
I'm sorry if I missed this in the talk,

01:48:32.560 --> 01:48:35.040
but what was the method of finding

01:48:35.040 --> 01:48:37.320
the kind of neutral-based capsid?

01:48:37.320 --> 01:48:38.640
Is it one that exists,

01:48:38.640 --> 01:48:42.280
or did you have to do a lot of editing to make it neutral?

01:48:42.840 --> 01:48:45.920
We first generated a rational library.

01:48:45.920 --> 01:48:47.120
It was a very tiny one.

01:48:47.120 --> 01:48:48.520
It was only 10 capsids.

01:48:49.800 --> 01:48:51.600
So, yeah, very rational.

01:48:52.440 --> 01:48:53.280
All right, okay, great.

01:48:53.280 --> 01:48:54.800
Thanks very much.

01:48:54.800 --> 01:48:55.880
And no question from Anna.

01:48:55.880 --> 01:48:56.720
Thank you.

01:48:56.720 --> 01:49:01.720
A capsid with zero tropism is fascinating in and of itself,

01:49:02.920 --> 01:49:04.600
but where is that blockade?

01:49:04.600 --> 01:49:07.200
Is it just not entering any cells?

01:49:07.200 --> 01:49:08.720
Is it not trafficking?

01:49:08.720 --> 01:49:11.640
Is it just coming out in the urine

01:49:11.640 --> 01:49:15.240
because it's not, where's the block?

01:49:15.240 --> 01:49:17.440
I was thinking that you can help me on that.

01:49:17.440 --> 01:49:19.880
When we look on the vector distribution,

01:49:19.880 --> 01:49:20.800
the copy numbers,

01:49:20.800 --> 01:49:24.360
we have not been able to find it anywhere, to be honest.

01:49:24.360 --> 01:49:26.480
We also perform a kinetic study in the sera

01:49:27.520 --> 01:49:30.880
to try to find if we were just circulating longer,

01:49:30.880 --> 01:49:33.160
but no, it's the same as AV9.

01:49:33.160 --> 01:49:35.000
And yeah, we are thinking about the urines,

01:49:35.000 --> 01:49:36.480
but we did not collect them.

01:49:36.480 --> 01:49:38.400
Yeah, very, great work.

01:49:38.400 --> 01:49:39.240
Thank you.

01:49:39.240 --> 01:49:40.080
Thank you, Anna.

01:49:41.680 --> 01:49:42.920
Sorry, sort of related to that.

01:49:42.920 --> 01:49:45.600
Have you looked at interactions of the base capsid

01:49:45.600 --> 01:49:47.720
with things like AVR?

01:49:47.720 --> 01:49:48.560
It's ongoing.

01:49:52.120 --> 01:49:55.240
Adrian, so have you looked into the shelf life,

01:49:55.240 --> 01:49:59.520
physical stability, aggregation profile of this new capsid?

01:49:59.520 --> 01:50:00.720
It's ongoing work,

01:50:00.720 --> 01:50:05.120
but the designs that we have performed on this capsid

01:50:05.120 --> 01:50:07.760
should not change the parental one property.

01:50:07.760 --> 01:50:10.120
So without disclosing the serotype,

01:50:10.120 --> 01:50:13.720
we are confident that everything will go well.

01:50:16.280 --> 01:50:18.880
Can you talk a little bit about the site specificity

01:50:18.880 --> 01:50:20.840
of your conjugation method?

01:50:20.840 --> 01:50:21.920
Sorry, can you repeat?

01:50:21.920 --> 01:50:23.160
Can you talk a little bit about

01:50:23.160 --> 01:50:26.240
whether your conjugation method is site specific?

01:50:26.240 --> 01:50:29.480
We are conjugating a natural amino acid

01:50:29.480 --> 01:50:31.200
that's a capsid surface.

01:50:31.200 --> 01:50:32.040
Thank you.

01:50:32.040 --> 01:50:33.680
You're welcome.

01:50:33.680 --> 01:50:35.000
Any more questions?

01:50:35.840 --> 01:50:40.840
If not, then I'll conclude the session here.

01:50:41.320 --> 01:50:43.120
And if you have time,

01:50:43.120 --> 01:50:45.320
please provide the feedback for the session.

01:50:45.320 --> 01:50:48.920
It is really helpful for the ASGCT for organization.

01:50:48.920 --> 01:50:50.480
And thank you all for coming.

